WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.840
Social media is changing our minds.

2
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.570
Social media acts like a drug.

3
00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.490
My guest today is Max Fisher.

4
00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.530
Max is a writer for the New York Times,

5
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.860
and he's the author of a brand new vitally important book

6
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.110
called The Chaos Machine.

7
00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.800
80% Of Americans are taking a drug 15 times a day

8
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.570
without realizing it's a drug.

9
00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.120
It's a referendum on all of the damage

10
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.850
currently being wrought by social media.

11
00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.070
Social media has much more power

12
00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.860
to affect how you think and how you feel

13
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.600
than you might believe.

14
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.730
I've become increasingly convinced

15
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.660
that the impact of social media and technology on our lives

16
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.550
and the lives of our children

17
00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:45.780
is truly one of the great existential issues of our time.

18
00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.100
Just spending time on it,

19
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.160
you inevitably end up not just serving the platform

20
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.950
when you're online, but in your offline life,

21
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.380
your emotional balance changes.

22
00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.150
I'm very excited to share with you

23
00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.090
what I think is a very important conversation.

24
00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.520
So please hit that Subscribe button.

25
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.989
And with that, I give you Max Fisher.

26
00:01:05.989 --> 00:01:08.739
(dramatic music)

27
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.480
Cool, so excited to have you here today

28
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.290
to talk about super important matters.

29
00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:26.910
So I applaud you for writing this book.

30
00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:31.910
It really is like the most comprehensive accounting

31
00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.000
of what's going on like from this forensic perspective

32
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.030
of how the internet is warping our minds,

33
00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.160
driving us apart from each other,

34
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.710
increasing radicalization and the like.

35
00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:47.070
And so I guess the first question I have for you is,

36
00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:50.100
it's an interesting subject matter for you to tackle

37
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:51.510
because you're not a tech reporter,

38
00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.790
you're like a foreign affairs reporter.

39
00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.770
You know, international affairs, et cetera.

40
00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.350
So talk a little bit about, you know,

41
00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.600
what brought you into this world

42
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.647
and why it captured you so to,

43
00:02:02.647 --> 00:02:06.210
you know, spend years writing, putting this book together.

44
00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:09.090
It wasn't something that I initially,

45
00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.500
I will be honest, took seriously.

46
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:14.013
I thought of social media as kind of this thing

47
00:02:14.013 --> 00:02:16.440
that was off to the side.

48
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.660
I know that after 2016,

49
00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.390
there was this kind of question out there about,

50
00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:22.710
did social media have something to do

51
00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.510
with the Trump phenomenon with the election?

52
00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.720
And I, maybe like a lot of people, somewhat naively thought,

53
00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.720
well, maybe it's kind of a neutral amplifier

54
00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:31.890
for things that are already out there.

55
00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.080
Maybe it exaggerates certain tendencies a little bit,

56
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.030
but it's just the internet, it's just a website.

57
00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.950
How influential could it possibly be?

58
00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.920
And it started to change for me

59
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.110
about a year after Trump's election

60
00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:46.140
when I was in me in Myanmar to report on the genocide there,

61
00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.210
which was this really horrible

62
00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:50.580
and very sudden explosion of violence

63
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.220
between the country's ethnic majority

64
00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.980
and an ethnic minority that partly was led by the state.

65
00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.860
But a lot of it was also ground up and spontaneous.

66
00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.990
And being there on the ground,

67
00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.630
I was far from the only person to notice

68
00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.610
that social media seemed to be playing

69
00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.370
some kind of a huge role in what was happening.

70
00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.650
In every conversation you would have with someone,

71
00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.060
whatever side they were on,

72
00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.010
they would always bring it back to social media.

73
00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:16.080
And even the United Nations,

74
00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.110
which it still blows my mind that United Nations said this.

75
00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.090
One of their top officials later said

76
00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.600
that Facebook had played a determining role in the genocide.

77
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.250
Not just in hosting hate speech,

78
00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.560
not just in being a platform

79
00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:29.820
that extremists have manipulated,

80
00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:33.690
but in actually driving it by what their systems promoted,

81
00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.180
and in how effective they were at inculcating

82
00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.120
so many people in that country into this point of view.

83
00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.990
And that started to really get my mind going

84
00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.570
because it felt, in a lot of ways,

85
00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.850
somewhat similar to what had happened in the United States,

86
00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:49.890
this kind of like social upheaval

87
00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.950
that seemed to be linked back to this technology.

88
00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:54.990
And because shortly after that,

89
00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:58.290
I started noticing that pretty much everywhere I went

90
00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.480
as a reporter for the Times, you know,

91
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.040
traveling around to different parts of the world,

92
00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.910
I would hear really similar stories to the Trump phenomenon,

93
00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:07.890
or to Myanmar that always seemed to link back

94
00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:08.723
to social media.

95
00:04:08.723 --> 00:04:09.630
And it would be smaller in scale,

96
00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.670
it would be a village that had erupted into violence.

97
00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:14.340
It would be a far right figure who had suddenly jumped

98
00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:15.300
from the fringes,

99
00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.480
to completely dominating their local society

100
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.490
based on social media.

101
00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.650
And that was when I started to think,

102
00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:24.750
this is more than just a bunch of websites.

103
00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:25.890
It's more than just a bunch of apps,

104
00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.710
it's tapping into and it's changing something

105
00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:29.760
really powerful.

106
00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.660
And these big instances that we're seeing

107
00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.510
are probably just the tip of the iceberg,

108
00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.910
because if it's something whose effects

109
00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:41.010
are being cycled through all of us

110
00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:43.500
at this very individual granular level,

111
00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.300
then these changes are probably way deeper

112
00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:46.140
than we appreciate.

113
00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:48.840
And that became this four or five year quest

114
00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.940
to try to understand

115
00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:52.680
as rigorously and empirically as I could

116
00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.480
what that actual effect is.

117
00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.480
Was Myanmar the first international flashpoint for this?

118
00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.880
I mean it sort of belies the idealistic promise

119
00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.550
of social media is born out by Arab Spring, right?

120
00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.400
It's like a mirror, you know, reflection of that,

121
00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.290
the opposite of it.

122
00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:11.910
But there is this sense

123
00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:14.790
or sort of a prevailing conventional wisdom

124
00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:19.320
that this is predominantly an American problem, right?

125
00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.360
Like just last week, the other day,

126
00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.480
Mark Zuckerberg on Joe Rogan's podcast,

127
00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.370
he was asked about polarization,

128
00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:27.960
and his response was that it,

129
00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.030
there was something uniquely American about it.

130
00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:33.120
And your book is this global adventure

131
00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.360
where you basically put that lie to the test.

132
00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:38.640
And it's very clear that this is,

133
00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.950
although it's, you know, manifesting,

134
00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:43.320
metastasizing in the United States,

135
00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.850
it's an international problem.

136
00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.440
Like you're in Germany, you're in Austria,

137
00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:48.000
you're in Myanmar, you're in Sri Lanka,

138
00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.280
like all of these places where you're seeing

139
00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:55.280
incidents of animosity, and violence, and polarization

140
00:05:55.620 --> 00:06:00.620
that can directly be tracked back to the algorithmic power

141
00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:05.550
of these, you know, mega social media platforms

142
00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:08.100
and how that's driving behavior.

143
00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.330
You really did read the book.

144
00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:10.500
I did, yeah.

145
00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:11.333
Most of it.

146
00:06:11.333 --> 00:06:12.900
I told you, I'm about halfway through it.

147
00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:16.353
I don't, well it's very flattering to hear it,

148
00:06:16.353 --> 00:06:17.430
hear that it resonated.

149
00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:21.600
So to my great shame, there were actually instances

150
00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.300
that I covered well before 2016,

151
00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.970
that in retrospect were exactly what you're saying,

152
00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:30.000
where these very similar patterns of polarization

153
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.890
and turning society against itself.

154
00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:36.210
There was this case in 2013 in India,

155
00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:39.060
so 100 years ago basically,

156
00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.460
that looked a lot like the Trump phenomenon

157
00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:42.570
and Myanmar combined,

158
00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:46.680
where it was rumors that started

159
00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.020
with a couple of very small accounts on Facebook

160
00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:51.930
about Muslims and Hindus,

161
00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:53.130
potentially threatening each other

162
00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.680
in one very remote part of the country.

163
00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.480
And the system, even that far back

164
00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:59.310
when it was much more rudimentary

165
00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:01.050
and much less powerful than it is now,

166
00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:02.760
identified that rumor as something

167
00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:04.680
that was gonna really drive engagement,

168
00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:06.780
and it was really going to excite users,

169
00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:08.850
and not just glue them to the platform,

170
00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:10.350
but get them to share the rumor

171
00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:12.300
and to get people, more people clicking.

172
00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.340
And so it pushed that out to huge parts of the country.

173
00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:15.300
And within a couple of weeks,

174
00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.520
that this cycle that is now very familiar

175
00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.680
of misinformation, leading to hate speech,

176
00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:20.790
leading to incitement,

177
00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:23.760
every step of it being promoted very assiduously

178
00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:24.630
by the platform,

179
00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:26.220
led to this explosion of violence,

180
00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:27.960
and I think tens of thousands of people

181
00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:28.830
pushed out of their homes,

182
00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:30.150
which if that had happened now,

183
00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.100
we would very readily identify this was something

184
00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:33.150
driven by social media,

185
00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:35.310
but it was a time, like you said,

186
00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:36.330
just after the Arab Spring,

187
00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:38.190
when we still thought of social media

188
00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:42.780
as something that was going to bring freedom and revolution.

189
00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:46.200
And the Obama administration actually stepped in

190
00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:47.790
to tell the Indian government

191
00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:49.740
which had blocked a bunch of the websites,

192
00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:50.940
that you need to unblock them

193
00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.830
because we believe in freedom and freedom of speech,

194
00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:54.990
which is a perfectly reasonable position 10 years ago,

195
00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:57.180
but now we would maybe look at a little bit differently.

196
00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:02.070
So maybe Mark Zuckerberg believes that polarization

197
00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:03.900
is something that is just happening in America,

198
00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:06.060
and what could his platform possibly have to do with it?

199
00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.280
But he has been presented,

200
00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.440
including by Facebook's own researchers

201
00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:12.540
with reams and reams of evidence,

202
00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.610
that its systems are not neutral

203
00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:15.780
and that it drives this behavior.

204
00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:16.613
Sure.

205
00:08:16.613 --> 00:08:19.080
I mean the book opens with this whistleblower

206
00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:20.910
that you name Jacob,

207
00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:22.867
who becomes kind of a harbinger of,

208
00:08:22.867 --> 00:08:24.990
you know, what's to unfold

209
00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:27.930
in terms of the scale of the problem

210
00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:31.500
and the degree of difficulty in tackling it.

211
00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:33.180
So talk a little bit about that.

212
00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:35.340
I mean that seems to also be, you know, some,

213
00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:36.630
you rooted this guy out

214
00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:40.680
and he becomes kind of a bit of a slight protagonist

215
00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:42.600
in terms of how you tell the story.

216
00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.130
He actually found me,

217
00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:47.040
and he, like a lot of people in the book

218
00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:48.960
who are the whistleblowers,

219
00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:50.457
whether they're in Silicon Valley, outside of it,

220
00:08:50.457 --> 00:08:52.170
the people who study the platforms,

221
00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:54.810
he was someone who started as a true believer.

222
00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:57.060
He lives in a country where,

223
00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:58.589
I'm trying to think about how much I can say

224
00:08:58.589 --> 00:08:59.422
about where he lives,

225
00:08:59.422 --> 00:09:01.500
'cause I don't want him to get, you know, identified,

226
00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:03.660
where it was very important to him

227
00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:04.980
that social media was there

228
00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:06.870
to bring the freedom and democracy

229
00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:08.820
that people in Silicon Valley were promising.

230
00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:10.260
And he really believed in that.

231
00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:14.310
His job was to click through posts on the platform to say,

232
00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:16.080
this breaks the rules, this doesn't break the rules,

233
00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:17.940
that Facebook and the other platforms

234
00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:19.410
employ thousands of these peoples

235
00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:23.910
to basically moderate and kind of manage the platforms.

236
00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:27.420
And he was really worried,

237
00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:30.210
because he saw that the hate and the lies

238
00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:31.043
and the misinformation

239
00:09:31.043 --> 00:09:32.880
seemed to be getting worse and worse all the time.

240
00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:35.310
And he had a fuzzy sense in the way that a lot of us did,

241
00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:38.177
but not proof that the system itself was driving it.

242
00:09:38.177 --> 00:09:42.570
And he also had this giant stack of documents

243
00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:45.420
that were Facebook's internal rule books

244
00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:47.340
for how to govern the platform.

245
00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:50.370
And he passed those on to me because he said,

246
00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:52.560
these are, they're nonsense, they're gibberish.

247
00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:54.390
A lot of them don't make sense, they don't fit together,

248
00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:56.070
there are mistakes in them.

249
00:09:56.070 --> 00:10:00.270
And it's not that the rules are nefarious.

250
00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:01.950
When I wrote a story for the Times on them,

251
00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:03.240
I think there were a lot of people looking for,

252
00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:05.280
well, they're, you know, trying to control free speech

253
00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:06.660
and trying to control politics.

254
00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:07.493
I think they're not,

255
00:10:07.493 --> 00:10:09.230
they're really just trying to tamp down,

256
00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:11.910
I mean, PR crises in a lot of cases,

257
00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:14.430
some of the documents say explicitly is the goal.

258
00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:17.190
But the attention and care in given to them

259
00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:20.100
was at an alarmingly low level,

260
00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:22.440
and so that was why he wanted me to broadcast them out

261
00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.810
so that the people in the company who he thought

262
00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:26.550
and really believed wanted to do the right thing

263
00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:28.620
would come in and save the day and fix them.

264
00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:32.700
Right, and so that was that article that you wrote in 2018

265
00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:35.880
for the Times, right, where you kind of exposed all of this,

266
00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:38.220
these manuals that are, you know,

267
00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:41.910
almost impossible to consume, let alone memorize,

268
