1504
01:00:58.920 --> 01:01:01.770
All right, let's get back to the show.

1505
01:01:01.770 --> 01:01:04.830
It's not just the post that you see on Facebook,

1506
01:01:04.830 --> 01:01:06.493
it's who shared the post, right?

1507
01:01:06.493 --> 01:01:07.860
Right, how they shared it.

1508
01:01:07.860 --> 01:01:08.970
Who is that person?

1509
01:01:08.970 --> 01:01:10.890
Do you identify with them?

1510
01:01:10.890 --> 01:01:14.070
And then this terrain that you get into in the book

1511
01:01:14.070 --> 01:01:15.862
about what are they called, super sharers?

1512
01:01:15.862 --> 01:01:16.695
Super posters.

1513
01:01:16.695 --> 01:01:18.270
Super posters, right?

1514
01:01:18.270 --> 01:01:21.240
Who have like this air of legitimacy,

1515
01:01:21.240 --> 01:01:23.730
like oh, I wanna align with that person,

1516
01:01:23.730 --> 01:01:26.160
I believe like they're credible in other areas.

1517
01:01:26.160 --> 01:01:27.600
So if they're sharing this,

1518
01:01:27.600 --> 01:01:28.950
I don't need to read the article,

1519
01:01:28.950 --> 01:01:32.040
I can read the headline and because I feel aligned with them

1520
01:01:32.040 --> 01:01:33.990
or I wanna be aligned with that person,

1521
01:01:33.990 --> 01:01:37.530
I will just reflexively reshare it.

1522
01:01:37.530 --> 01:01:38.363
Right.

1523
01:01:38.363 --> 01:01:39.540
Can I tell you about the school bullying thing

1524
01:01:39.540 --> 01:01:40.373
that kind of led me to that?

1525
01:01:40.373 --> 01:01:42.780
'Cause this unlocked so much for me,

1526
01:01:42.780 --> 01:01:45.870
and I feel like helped me really understand

1527
01:01:45.870 --> 01:01:48.990
how social media can change your sense of morality

1528
01:01:48.990 --> 01:01:49.823
and right and wrong.

1529
01:01:49.823 --> 01:01:51.300
So I was working on a story

1530
01:01:51.300 --> 01:01:54.000
that was related to this research

1531
01:01:54.000 --> 01:01:55.710
that areas with higher Facebook usage

1532
01:01:55.710 --> 01:01:58.620
saw more racist attacks on refugees basically.

1533
01:01:58.620 --> 01:02:00.990
And I was trying to figure out, is that, could that be true?

1534
01:02:00.990 --> 01:02:02.550
And could social media actually change

1535
01:02:02.550 --> 01:02:03.690
your sense of right and wrong,

1536
01:02:03.690 --> 01:02:05.550
which is something that I think a lot of us suspected,

1537
01:02:05.550 --> 01:02:08.250
but how can you actually demonstrate that?

1538
01:02:08.250 --> 01:02:12.150
And I went to this woman named Betsy Levy Pollock,

1539
01:02:12.150 --> 01:02:14.190
who's a researcher, I think at Princeton,

1540
01:02:14.190 --> 01:02:16.530
and she's got a genius grant, and she's a very smart lady.

1541
01:02:16.530 --> 01:02:20.370
And I said, how would you try to go about figuring this out?

1542
01:02:20.370 --> 01:02:22.890
And she had worked a lot in like Rwanda and Genicera,

1543
01:02:22.890 --> 01:02:24.210
which I thought she would wanna talk to me about,

1544
01:02:24.210 --> 01:02:27.420
but she said actually, the biggest comparable case here

1545
01:02:27.420 --> 01:02:28.800
is school bullying.

1546
01:02:28.800 --> 01:02:30.450
This really interesting study that she did,

1547
01:02:30.450 --> 01:02:32.550
where she went to a bunch of high schools,

1548
01:02:33.630 --> 01:02:36.060
and she had this theory

1549
01:02:36.060 --> 01:02:39.390
that people determined whether or not they think bullying

1550
01:02:39.390 --> 01:02:43.290
is okay to do, not based on any internal moral compass,

1551
01:02:43.290 --> 01:02:45.420
or not based on what their teachers tell them,

1552
01:02:45.420 --> 01:02:48.780
but by what they think their peers collectively believe.

1553
01:02:48.780 --> 01:02:51.870
But they can't, you can't put out a poll if you're a kid,

1554
01:02:51.870 --> 01:02:53.790
to know what do all of your peers believe?

1555
01:02:53.790 --> 01:02:55.050
So she said that she thinks

1556
01:02:55.050 --> 01:02:57.240
the shorthand people were using was,

1557
01:02:57.240 --> 01:02:59.100
trying to spot the few people in their community

1558
01:02:59.100 --> 01:03:00.990
who seem to be the most influential

1559
01:03:00.990 --> 01:03:03.840
and the most visible, judge what they think about bullying,

1560
01:03:03.840 --> 01:03:07.380
and then they would mirror their internal beliefs,

1561
01:03:07.380 --> 01:03:09.420
their sense of morality, right and wrong off of that.

1562
01:03:09.420 --> 01:03:14.357
And she tested this by identifying the like 20 kids

1563
01:03:15.270 --> 01:03:16.470
out of like every 1,000,

1564
01:03:16.470 --> 01:03:19.650
a really small number who seemed to be the most visible

1565
01:03:19.650 --> 01:03:21.510
and who had been,

1566
01:03:21.510 --> 01:03:22.980
I guess you would say supportive of bullying.

1567
01:03:22.980 --> 01:03:25.980
Maybe they were bullying people on Instagram, online,

1568
01:03:25.980 --> 01:03:29.910
and got them to just stand up and say to their community,

1569
01:03:29.910 --> 01:03:32.190
in some context, I actually think bullying is wrong,

1570
01:03:32.190 --> 01:03:33.750
and I don't think that you should do it.

1571
01:03:33.750 --> 01:03:35.070
And just doing this,

1572
01:03:35.070 --> 01:03:38.760
just having the influential kind of norm setters

1573
01:03:38.760 --> 01:03:42.060
in the community change what they were conveying out

1574
01:03:42.060 --> 01:03:42.893
to the rest of the community,

1575
01:03:42.893 --> 01:03:45.660
completely flipped everyone in the school.

1576
01:03:45.660 --> 01:03:48.120
And not only was there much less bullying after that,

1577
01:03:48.120 --> 01:03:50.130
but the surveys that she would take of the students

1578
01:03:50.130 --> 01:03:52.980
found that they internally had come to think

1579
01:03:52.980 --> 01:03:53.940
that bullying was wrong.

1580
01:03:53.940 --> 01:03:57.480
So this is this idea of social reference

1581
01:03:57.480 --> 01:04:00.690
that we derive our norms from a few influential people

1582
01:04:00.690 --> 01:04:03.000
that we used to infer the whole community.

1583
01:04:03.000 --> 01:04:06.960
And on social media, the way that those social reference

1584
01:04:06.960 --> 01:04:10.560
get picked out is the platforms designate

1585
01:04:10.560 --> 01:04:13.680
a few people who they believe

1586
01:04:13.680 --> 01:04:17.070
their systems believe are going to be very engaging to us.

1587
01:04:17.070 --> 01:04:18.390
These are super posters,

1588
01:04:18.390 --> 01:04:21.900
and it puts those people in front of us over and over again.

1589
01:04:21.900 --> 01:04:24.150
I bet if you opened up your Twitter feed,

1590
01:04:24.150 --> 01:04:27.390
the algorithm would show a few people on the top

1591
01:04:27.390 --> 01:04:28.223
over and over again.

1592
01:04:28.223 --> 01:04:29.070
100%, yeah.

1593
01:04:29.070 --> 01:04:31.140
'Cause those are the people who you engage with, right?

1594
01:04:31.140 --> 01:04:33.420
I mean, same thing happens on my feed.

1595
01:04:33.420 --> 01:04:35.820
And it's a few people who the system is just learned,

1596
01:04:35.820 --> 01:04:37.380
I look at their tweets a little bit longer,

1597
01:04:37.380 --> 01:04:39.390
maybe I'm like to like it or reply to it,

1598
01:04:39.390 --> 01:04:41.550
these are people who I pay attention to.

1599
01:04:41.550 --> 01:04:44.880
And it's going to surface posts from those people

1600
01:04:44.880 --> 01:04:49.650
and those individuals who deliver the type of content

1601
01:04:49.650 --> 01:04:53.190
that is most engaging, which means the most outraged,

1602
01:04:53.190 --> 01:04:55.620
which means the most kind of us versus them,

1603
01:04:55.620 --> 01:04:57.150
the most tribal.

1604
01:04:57.150 --> 01:05:01.980
And these are also people who have been studied a little bit

1605
01:05:01.980 --> 01:05:03.360
in the last two years,

1606
01:05:03.360 --> 01:05:05.940
and kind of their psychological temperament.

1607
01:05:05.940 --> 01:05:08.640
And it's a little concerning.

1608
01:05:08.640 --> 01:05:10.530
They're basically bullies themselves.

1609
01:05:10.530 --> 01:05:11.820
They have a few tendencies.

1610
01:05:11.820 --> 01:05:13.710
Grandiose narcissism is one of them,

1611
01:05:13.710 --> 01:05:15.690
which is associated with insecurity.

1612
01:05:15.690 --> 01:05:18.000
And that's why these people use social media a lot

1613
01:05:18.000 --> 01:05:20.640
because they really need the validation.

1614
01:05:20.640 --> 01:05:23.610
Something called negative social potency,

1615
01:05:23.610 --> 01:05:25.650
which means someone who derives pleasure

1616
01:05:25.650 --> 01:05:28.410
from seeing other people in pain.

1617
01:05:28.410 --> 01:05:30.240
This is also something that plays really well

1618
01:05:30.240 --> 01:05:31.073
in social media,

1619
01:05:31.073 --> 01:05:33.810
because if you're someone who goes on and yells at like,

1620
01:05:33.810 --> 01:05:36.360
hey, look at this idiot, look at what this person said,

1621
01:05:36.360 --> 01:05:38.430
that's something that will drive a lot of engagement.

1622
01:05:38.430 --> 01:05:42.120
So these are the people who offline, are not influential.

1623
01:05:42.120 --> 01:05:43.680
And I went and interviewed a few of these people

1624
01:05:43.680 --> 01:05:46.200
who were elevated by the platforms,

1625
01:05:46.200 --> 01:05:49.290
and they're not basement dwellers,

1626
01:05:49.290 --> 01:05:50.400
they're just like regular people

1627
01:05:50.400 --> 01:05:51.840
who happen to be really hiked or active,

1628
01:05:51.840 --> 01:05:54.120
and a little bit mean on social media,

1629
01:05:54.120 --> 01:05:57.780
but they set the norms for these entire communities.

1630
01:05:57.780 --> 01:06:00.480
Yeah, and that's something that is true

1631
01:06:00.480 --> 01:06:01.907
on the right and the left.

1632
01:06:01.907 --> 01:06:02.740
Absolutely.

1633
01:06:02.740 --> 01:06:04.410
Like if you're looking at the political sphere,

1634
01:06:04.410 --> 01:06:09.410
there is similar like strategy or tactics that are at play,

1635
01:06:09.540 --> 01:06:12.270
like they're Shot and Freud at like,

1636
01:06:12.270 --> 01:06:15.765
oh, pointing out like the misstep of the out,

1637
01:06:15.765 --> 01:06:18.930
you know, the party that they're making fun of, et cetera.

1638
01:06:18.930 --> 01:06:21.840
Sometimes it's mean spirited, sometimes it's comedy.

1639
01:06:21.840 --> 01:06:26.840
It's, you know, just a ferocity of volume,

1640
01:06:27.210 --> 01:06:29.793
you know, like it's lots of posts, right?

1641
01:06:31.891 --> 01:06:33.240
And it's interesting what,

1642
01:06:33.240 --> 01:06:35.220
no matter what silo you go into,

1643
01:06:35.220 --> 01:06:37.110
it's those are the people that rise to the top

1644
01:06:37.110 --> 01:06:38.850
and that the algorithm is gonna elevate.

1645
01:06:38.850 --> 01:06:40.140
And they do, they set the tone.

1646
01:06:40.140 --> 01:06:42.960
Like, oh, this is what's acceptable in this discourse

1647
01:06:42.960 --> 01:06:45.240
to be part of this in group,

1648
01:06:45.240 --> 01:06:48.960
and to signal your membership in good standing

1649
01:06:48.960 --> 01:06:52.770
by resharing it or doing your version of that post.

1650
01:06:52.770 --> 01:06:55.620
Right, and these are people who, in the offline world,

1651
01:06:55.620 --> 01:06:57.960
if they're especially noisy or kind of obnoxious,

1652
01:06:57.960 --> 01:06:59.880
would be maybe shunned,

1653
01:06:59.880 --> 01:07:01.950
or maybe would not be that influential.

1654
01:07:01.950 --> 01:07:04.950
But it is behavior that serves the algorithms

1655
01:07:04.950 --> 01:07:06.120
rather than the social norm,

1656
01:07:06.120 --> 01:07:08.880
so it's what gets elevated.

1657
01:07:08.880 --> 01:07:09.720
And it's something that happens,

1658
01:07:09.720 --> 01:07:11.220
not just in political spheres,

1659
01:07:11.220 --> 01:07:14.370
but one of the big cases that I talk about in the book

1660
01:07:14.370 --> 01:07:19.200
is this happening in basically mom blog groups,

1661
01:07:19.200 --> 01:07:21.720
and where this leads over time,

1662
01:07:21.720 --> 01:07:26.010
where you have these forums of young parents

1663
01:07:26.010 --> 01:07:26.940
who are sharing information,

1664
01:07:26.940 --> 01:07:27.960
and this was like gamers,

1665
01:07:27.960 --> 01:07:30.750
one of the first really big communities to migrate online

1666
01:07:30.750 --> 01:07:33.510
because you have a lot of gen X moms who are,

1667
01:07:33.510 --> 01:07:35.640
you know, trying to figure out this parenting thing,

1668
01:07:35.640 --> 01:07:36.473
it seems hard.

1669
01:07:38.550 --> 01:07:40.530
That the people who would rise in that community

1670
01:07:40.530 --> 01:07:44.430
were the people who were conspiratorial,

1671
01:07:44.430 --> 01:07:48.360
who had the kind of sharpest criticism of doctors,

1672
01:07:48.360 --> 01:07:50.580
or of government policy around childcare,

1673
01:07:50.580 --> 01:07:54.540
and where this ultimately ended up leading over time

1674
01:07:54.540 --> 01:07:55.920
was towards anti-vaxxers,

1675
01:07:55.920 --> 01:07:58.740
which grew out of these mom Facebook groups,

1676
01:07:58.740 --> 01:07:59.940
mom YouTube channels,

1677
01:07:59.940 --> 01:08:03.420
because it was the end point

1678
01:08:03.420 --> 01:08:05.670
of all of that kind of negative social potency,

1679
01:08:05.670 --> 01:08:09.330
and this tilt towards conspiracy, conspiracism and extremism

1680
01:08:09.330 --> 01:08:11.010
that would get promoted on the pages.

1681
01:08:11.010 --> 01:08:12.600
Right, and Renée DiResta

1682
01:08:12.600 --> 01:08:14.340
being kind of the tip of the spear

1683
01:08:14.340 --> 01:08:16.237
and rooting that out, and helping to,

1684
01:08:16.237 --> 01:08:20.100
you know, people to really understand how that transpired.

1685
01:08:20.100 --> 01:08:22.530
Yeah, I was very happy to talk to her a lot in the book.

1686
01:08:22.530 --> 01:08:25.080
And she is someone else who started as a real true believer.

1687
01:08:25.080 --> 01:08:27.720
I mean, she was an investor in Silicon Valley.

1688
01:08:27.720 --> 01:08:30.210
She was going to the conferences.

1689
01:08:30.210 --> 01:08:31.980
She was involved with some of these companies.

1690
01:08:31.980 --> 01:08:34.920
And she started to see this kind of interesting parallel.

1691
01:08:34.920 --> 01:08:36.330
On the one hand she was online,

1692
01:08:36.330 --> 01:08:38.580
and she would see what the hell is going on?

1693
01:08:38.580 --> 01:08:39.660
These Facebook groups,

1694
01:08:39.660 --> 01:08:41.970
all of a sudden have crazy anti-vaccine sentiment

1695
01:08:41.970 --> 01:08:44.130
that is a complete minority offline,

1696
01:08:44.130 --> 01:08:46.500
but is totally dominant online,

1697
01:08:46.500 --> 01:08:48.750
which she later figured out was because

1698
01:08:48.750 --> 01:08:50.250
it just performed so well.

1699
01:08:50.250 --> 01:08:52.350
And at the same time, she's in Silicon Valley

1700
01:08:52.350 --> 01:08:54.690
and she's going to these investment conferences,

1701
01:08:54.690 --> 01:08:56.310
and she's saying, well, why do these companies

1702
01:08:56.310 --> 01:08:58.920
that have no revenue model,

1703
01:08:58.920 --> 01:09:00.870
that have no way to bring in any money,

1704
01:09:00.870 --> 01:09:03.150
but just say, oh, I'm gonna get a billion users

1705
01:09:03.150 --> 01:09:04.110
and then sell ads against it,

1706
01:09:04.110 --> 01:09:05.730
why are they getting all of the investment money?

1707
01:09:05.730 --> 01:09:07.380
So it was funny to talk to her,

1708
01:09:07.380 --> 01:09:09.420
to see these kind of two things converging in once

1709
01:09:09.420 --> 01:09:10.253
and where it ended up playing.

1710
01:09:10.253 --> 01:09:12.840
Right, and of course, similarly,

1711
01:09:12.840 --> 01:09:16.830
this gets played out in all kinds of other issues.

1712
01:09:16.830 --> 01:09:21.270
You talk about the, you know,

1713
01:09:21.270 --> 01:09:26.270
the anti-immigration situation is Germany or Austria.

1714
01:09:26.430 --> 01:09:27.263
Germany, yeah.

1715
01:09:27.263 --> 01:09:29.220
Yeah, where, you know,

1716
01:09:29.220 --> 01:09:31.680
there's this sense if you're online,

1717
01:09:31.680 --> 01:09:34.773
that there is a tremendous amount of acrimony,

1718
01:09:36.540 --> 01:09:38.790
you know, towards these communities of people

1719
01:09:38.790 --> 01:09:41.250
that doesn't really represent accurately

1720
01:09:41.250 --> 01:09:44.100
the true sentiment of the greater population,

1721
01:09:44.100 --> 01:09:46.740
but ultimately ends up driving a lot of,

1722
01:09:46.740 --> 01:09:48.960
you know, really bad behavior.

1723
01:09:48.960 --> 01:09:51.510
Yeah, it was wild going to these towns,

1724
01:09:51.510 --> 01:09:53.580
and this is a story I did for the Times at the time,

1725
01:09:53.580 --> 01:09:55.290
and it's a chapter in the book

1726
01:09:55.290 --> 01:10:00.053
where everything in the town would seem great.

1727
01:10:01.410 --> 01:10:03.210
Like people were really friendly towards,

1728
01:10:03.210 --> 01:10:04.770
there would be big refugee populations,

1729
01:10:04.770 --> 01:10:06.600
'cause this is like 2015, 2016

1730
01:10:06.600 --> 01:10:08.130
when a lot of refugees had just come

1731
01:10:08.130 --> 01:10:09.600
from Syria, Iraq, and Afghanistan.

1732
01:10:09.600 --> 01:10:12.000
So there's a lot of resettlement happening in small towns.

1733
01:10:12.000 --> 01:10:14.610
And if you went into the city streets,

1734
01:10:14.610 --> 01:10:16.230
people were really nice to them,

1735
01:10:16.230 --> 01:10:18.600
they would be helping them to get groceries.

1736
01:10:18.600 --> 01:10:20.100
You would go to these community centers

1737
01:10:20.100 --> 01:10:21.660
that were really well funded.

1738
01:10:21.660 --> 01:10:23.490
But then if you would go online,

1739
01:10:23.490 --> 01:10:25.440
or talk to people who would go online,

1740
01:10:25.440 --> 01:10:26.940
it was just completely flipped.

1741
01:10:26.940 --> 01:10:30.780
And it was just this racist, hatred, and vitriol.

1742
01:10:30.780 --> 01:10:33.090
And I thought what was so striking about that

1743
01:10:33.090 --> 01:10:34.740
is it was a reminder of,

1744
01:10:34.740 --> 01:10:36.960
despite what Mark Zuckerberg might say,

1745
01:10:36.960 --> 01:10:40.740
the platforms are not actually neutral shows

1746
01:10:40.740 --> 01:10:42.240
of what is already in the community,

1747
01:10:42.240 --> 01:10:44.820
or maybe it picks out something that is there,

1748
01:10:44.820 --> 01:10:46.920
but it's kind of small, and it's on the fringes,

1749
01:10:46.920 --> 01:10:49.410
and it blasts it out and widens it out

1750
01:10:49.410 --> 01:10:53.790
because online social norms that say refugees are great

1751
01:10:53.790 --> 01:10:56.370
and we accept them is not going to win that much engagement.

1752
01:10:56.370 --> 01:10:57.840
But something that says,

1753
01:10:57.840 --> 01:10:59.700
you have something to fear in your community,

1754
01:10:59.700 --> 01:11:01.350
these new neighbors are really scary,

1755
01:11:01.350 --> 01:11:02.820
and you should be really upset about them,

1756
01:11:02.820 --> 01:11:05.040
it's going to surface every time.

1757
01:11:05.040 --> 01:11:06.660
Sure, and that's not just opinion,

1758
01:11:06.660 --> 01:11:09.660
because it was being fueled by fake news stories about,

1759
01:11:09.660 --> 01:11:12.150
you know, rapes, and crimes and all that kind of stuff

1760
01:11:12.150 --> 01:11:14.040
that actually wasn't factually correct.

1761
01:11:14.040 --> 01:11:16.020
Right, yes, it was a lot of misinformation

1762
01:11:16.020 --> 01:11:18.840
that would go viral because it would kind of hook in

1763
01:11:18.840 --> 01:11:20.970
to the sense of fear and the sense of identity threat.

1764
01:11:20.970 --> 01:11:23.460
And it would lead to violence.

1765
01:11:23.460 --> 01:11:25.860
There were a few cases that I talk about in the book

1766
01:11:25.860 --> 01:11:28.260
of people who were fine,

1767
01:11:28.260 --> 01:11:30.210
and then would start spending a lot of time online.

1768
01:11:30.210 --> 01:11:31.830
Maybe weren't even political,

1769
01:11:31.830 --> 01:11:34.380
and would start to drift towards these ideas

1770
01:11:34.380 --> 01:11:37.350
because they felt online, like it's the norm,

1771
01:11:37.350 --> 01:11:39.930
and it's accepted and this is how everybody feels.

1772
01:11:39.930 --> 01:11:42.210
And they're doing, they're serving the greater good

1773
01:11:42.210 --> 01:11:44.970
because they're solving this problem on behalf of everybody

1774
01:11:44.970 --> 01:11:47.580
which goes to that group identity piece.

1775
01:11:47.580 --> 01:11:51.090
And you know, suddenly having a purpose,

1776
01:11:51.090 --> 01:11:54.240
you know, some meaning in your life to redress,

1777
01:11:54.240 --> 01:11:56.790
what they legitimately feel to be a harm.

1778
01:11:56.790 --> 01:11:59.760
Right, it's actualizing.

1779
01:11:59.760 --> 01:12:02.370
It gives you an identity, something to hold onto.

1780
01:12:02.370 --> 01:12:05.610
And if you talk to people who kind of fall down that,

1781
01:12:05.610 --> 01:12:07.620
they're often legitimately surprised

1782
01:12:07.620 --> 01:12:10.500
that their real, actual real life community

1783
01:12:10.500 --> 01:12:12.660
is not gonna greet them as champions and heroes

1784
01:12:12.660 --> 01:12:14.160
for what they're doing.

1785
01:12:14.160 --> 01:12:16.680
And when you become so invested in that,

1786
01:12:16.680 --> 01:12:20.130
it becomes very difficult to disabuse people of it,

1787
01:12:20.130 --> 01:12:22.890
no matter what facts you put in front of them.

1788
01:12:22.890 --> 01:12:24.180
Like you see this with QAnon,

1789
01:12:24.180 --> 01:12:27.170
there's still people holding onto this idea, right?

1790
01:12:27.170 --> 01:12:30.433
Right, with QAnon, it's sad because it's,

1791
01:12:30.433 --> 01:12:32.400
I mean, for a lot of reasons obviously,

1792
01:12:32.400 --> 01:12:35.073
but a lot of them really are just lonely.

1793
01:12:36.240 --> 01:12:37.710
Well, that was the big piece there

1794
01:12:37.710 --> 01:12:39.090
was the community piece.

1795
01:12:39.090 --> 01:12:41.970
And the gamification of all of it that made it

1796
01:12:41.970 --> 01:12:45.510
this mass group, you know, activity

1797
01:12:45.510 --> 01:12:46.620
where they could get together

1798
01:12:46.620 --> 01:12:48.360
and try to solve these puzzles.

1799
01:12:48.360 --> 01:12:49.350
Right, right.

1800
01:12:49.350 --> 01:12:50.490
It gives you a purpose,

1801
01:12:50.490 --> 01:12:52.860
it helps you make sense of the world.

1802
01:12:52.860 --> 01:12:54.930
And it helps you understand

1803
01:12:54.930 --> 01:12:56.220
if something bad had happened to you,

1804
01:12:56.220 --> 01:12:58.830
maybe you'd lost your home, you been displaced,

1805
01:12:58.830 --> 01:13:02.040
you're alienated from a family member.

1806
01:13:02.040 --> 01:13:03.720
It's all part of this larger struggle

1807
01:13:03.720 --> 01:13:05.520
where you can find all of these other people

1808
01:13:05.520 --> 01:13:06.353
to help you with it.

1809
01:13:06.353 --> 01:13:11.250
But it started with, this is like the one,

1810
01:13:11.250 --> 01:13:13.410
I tried not to hold back any reporting for the book,

1811
01:13:13.410 --> 01:13:16.230
but there was one thing that I just didn't get a chance

1812
01:13:16.230 --> 01:13:18.030
to put into an article that I put into the book,

1813
01:13:18.030 --> 01:13:21.060
which is that the origins of QAnon

1814
01:13:21.060 --> 01:13:23.460
is of course it's the Pizzagate conspiracy theory,

1815
01:13:23.460 --> 01:13:26.310
which is, and again, it sounds so crazy

1816
01:13:26.310 --> 01:13:27.143
when you say it out loud,

1817
01:13:27.143 --> 01:13:30.420
it's the idea that Democratic elites

1818
01:13:30.420 --> 01:13:34.290
are harvesting the blood and the organs of children,

1819
01:13:34.290 --> 01:13:36.030
sounds so stupid.

1820
