01:44:58.020 --> 01:44:59.580
which I think is partly why that incident

2590
01:44:59.580 --> 01:45:01.200
has been kind of forgotten.

2591
01:45:01.200 --> 01:45:03.450
I mean the New York Times ran like 10 articles about it.

2592
01:45:03.450 --> 01:45:07.350
Yeah, and for every story like Cecil, or the bird watcher,

2593
01:45:07.350 --> 01:45:08.790
there's a million other ones, right?

2594
01:45:08.790 --> 01:45:11.190
So here we have this problem.

2595
01:45:11.190 --> 01:45:14.910
This problem's not going away, the weaponization of speech,

2596
01:45:14.910 --> 01:45:19.897
the policing of speech, the mob unleashed, the doxxing.

2597
01:45:19.897 --> 01:45:23.310
You know, there's clear cases where we can all,

2598
01:45:23.310 --> 01:45:26.580
maybe not all of us, but like reasonable minds would say,

2599
01:45:26.580 --> 01:45:27.960
this is probably not good.

2600
01:45:27.960 --> 01:45:29.580
Too far.
We should, yeah.

2601
01:45:29.580 --> 01:45:30.840
But where do we draw that line?

2602
01:45:30.840 --> 01:45:33.150
And once you get into drawing those lines,

2603
01:45:33.150 --> 01:45:35.820
it becomes infinitely problematic.

2604
01:45:35.820 --> 01:45:39.780
So this is the problem that all of these founders

2605
01:45:39.780 --> 01:45:40.680
are grappling with.

2606
01:45:40.680 --> 01:45:42.390
This is the problem that we're grappling with,

2607
01:45:42.390 --> 01:45:44.040
this is society.

2608
01:45:44.040 --> 01:45:46.380
I don't see it getting any easier to solve.

2609
01:45:46.380 --> 01:45:49.980
Like where do you come down in terms of your thoughts

2610
01:45:49.980 --> 01:45:54.660
on the best way to manage and rectify

2611
01:45:54.660 --> 01:45:56.640
what is wildly spinning out of control?

2612
01:45:56.640 --> 01:45:58.083
I think that it's,

2613
01:46:00.450 --> 01:46:03.960
I think it starts with asking a slightly different question,

2614
01:46:03.960 --> 01:46:06.543
or kind of reframing the question in our minds.

2615
01:46:08.040 --> 01:46:12.090
The kind of the version of the question of, you know,

2616
01:46:12.090 --> 01:46:13.770
what's the amount of outrage that we allow,

2617
01:46:13.770 --> 01:46:15.930
or what are the posts that we allow or don't allow,

2618
01:46:15.930 --> 01:46:18.930
it's a little bit like thinking,

2619
01:46:18.930 --> 01:46:21.060
where do we put all of this toxic sludge

2620
01:46:21.060 --> 01:46:23.400
that's coming out of this factory, that's in our backyard?

2621
01:46:23.400 --> 01:46:26.700
Should we put it in drums, or should we put it in, you know,

2622
01:46:26.700 --> 01:46:28.410
plastic containers?

2623
01:46:28.410 --> 01:46:29.431
Or should we--

2624
01:46:29.431 --> 01:46:30.264
Go dump 'em in the bay?

2625
01:46:30.264 --> 01:46:31.890
Right, yeah, or go dump it in the bay.

2626
01:46:31.890 --> 01:46:35.730
And another way to think about it is,

2627
01:46:35.730 --> 01:46:38.130
should the factory be producing this much toxic sludge?

2628
01:46:38.130 --> 01:46:40.770
Is there a way that we can maybe just dial things back

2629
01:46:40.770 --> 01:46:43.893
in the factory so that it is not polluting our homes?

2630
01:46:45.630 --> 01:46:48.480
And I think that those questions get a lot easier to solve

2631
01:46:48.480 --> 01:46:50.790
when you think of it less in terms of

2632
01:46:50.790 --> 01:46:53.070
how do we manage the outputs of the system,

2633
01:46:53.070 --> 01:46:56.130
and more in terms of how do we get this system

2634
01:46:56.130 --> 01:46:59.220
to stop pumping out the sludge?

2635
01:46:59.220 --> 01:47:03.210
And that's partly a high level regulatory question,

2636
01:47:03.210 --> 01:47:06.240
which there's more movement on than you might think.

2637
01:47:06.240 --> 01:47:07.920
There's a real chance that some of these platforms

2638
01:47:07.920 --> 01:47:09.870
might get kicked out of big parts of the EU.

2639
01:47:09.870 --> 01:47:11.220
And that's something that could really happen.

2640
01:47:11.220 --> 01:47:12.120
Wow.

2641
01:47:12.120 --> 01:47:14.580
What gives you that sense?

2642
01:47:14.580 --> 01:47:18.240
The regulators there have imposed

2643
01:47:18.240 --> 01:47:21.090
some pretty strict fines.

2644
01:47:21.090 --> 01:47:24.360
The social attitudes towards the platform

2645
01:47:24.360 --> 01:47:26.670
are a little bit different, especially in Germany,

2646
01:47:26.670 --> 01:47:29.343
which is a big deciding vote in a lot of EU matters.

2647
01:47:30.210 --> 01:47:33.840
There is really a sense that this is like this foreign thing

2648
01:47:33.840 --> 01:47:35.580
that has come into our country is polluting it.

2649
01:47:35.580 --> 01:47:37.800
So it feels easier to kind of say,

2650
01:47:37.800 --> 01:47:38.820
we should just get rid of it.

2651
01:47:38.820 --> 01:47:41.160
But I think if it does happen,

2652
01:47:41.160 --> 01:47:44.310
the way it would happen would be imposing rules

2653
01:47:44.310 --> 01:47:46.560
that are impossible for the platforms to follow

2654
01:47:46.560 --> 01:47:49.560
without fundamentally changing their nature.

2655
01:47:49.560 --> 01:47:51.390
And you've already started to see the start of this,

2656
01:47:51.390 --> 01:47:55.920
where you see German laws that Facebook is legally culpable

2657
01:47:55.920 --> 01:47:57.240
for certain kinds of speech

2658
01:47:57.240 --> 01:47:59.910
if they don't preemptively remove it,

2659
01:47:59.910 --> 01:48:01.800
which is not possible under their current model

2660
01:48:01.800 --> 01:48:05.310
where the posts go out and they review it after the fact.

2661
01:48:05.310 --> 01:48:06.660
And then I think the choice will be

2662
01:48:06.660 --> 01:48:09.660
this kind of game of chicken, where the EU will say,

2663
01:48:09.660 --> 01:48:10.500
well, you can stay,

2664
01:48:10.500 --> 01:48:12.960
but you have to completely re-engineer your platforms

2665
01:48:12.960 --> 01:48:14.370
to meet the standards that we set.

2666
01:48:14.370 --> 01:48:17.310
And then these companies will say, well, you know,

2667
01:48:17.310 --> 01:48:19.710
Europe is a shrinking market for us anyway, which is true,

2668
01:48:19.710 --> 01:48:21.390
because adoption rates are going down,

2669
01:48:21.390 --> 01:48:23.940
and population rates in Europe

2670
01:48:23.940 --> 01:48:26.100
are pretty static or declining.

2671
01:48:26.100 --> 01:48:27.810
And our future is in the developing world,

2672
01:48:27.810 --> 01:48:29.490
so we'll you see later.

2673
01:48:29.490 --> 01:48:30.330
Right.

2674
01:48:30.330 --> 01:48:32.190
But that would also set up

2675
01:48:32.190 --> 01:48:34.440
a sort of Cold War domino thing, right?

2676
01:48:34.440 --> 01:48:36.900
Like if Europe falls, what's next?

2677
01:48:36.900 --> 01:48:38.820
They've set a precedent.

2678
01:48:38.820 --> 01:48:42.480
You know, my mind goes towards what happens

2679
01:48:42.480 --> 01:48:44.160
when every country sets up

2680
01:48:44.160 --> 01:48:46.440
different laws and regulations and rules

2681
01:48:46.440 --> 01:48:51.150
around how these entities can operate in their territory,

2682
01:48:51.150 --> 01:48:53.460
which becomes impossible,

2683
01:48:53.460 --> 01:48:55.320
because these are global entities, right,

2684
01:48:55.320 --> 01:48:56.970
so how are they gonna, you know,

2685
01:48:56.970 --> 01:49:00.420
sort of partition how they're managing it

2686
01:49:00.420 --> 01:49:03.720
based upon, you know, geographical boundaries.

2687
01:49:03.720 --> 01:49:06.720
Right, and the companies have started to get

2688
01:49:06.720 --> 01:49:08.910
pretty aggressive about fighting back.

2689
01:49:08.910 --> 01:49:10.380
Do you remember this thing in Australia?

2690
01:49:10.380 --> 01:49:11.980
I think it was about a year ago,

2691
01:49:12.900 --> 01:49:16.860
the Australian government was setting these new rules.

2692
01:49:16.860 --> 01:49:18.690
Oh God, what was it?

2693
01:49:18.690 --> 01:49:20.250
Oh, it was Facebook had to,

2694
01:49:20.250 --> 01:49:24.840
Facebook and Google both had to sign agreements

2695
01:49:24.840 --> 01:49:27.990
with major Australian media companies

2696
01:49:27.990 --> 01:49:30.090
to basically say, we Facebook, or we Google,

2697
01:49:30.090 --> 01:49:31.680
will give you a portion of ad revenue

2698
01:49:31.680 --> 01:49:33.810
because we are selling ads against your content.

2699
01:49:33.810 --> 01:49:34.643
Right.

2700
01:49:34.643 --> 01:49:36.420
Which was, there are differing views

2701
01:49:36.420 --> 01:49:37.560
on whether or not that was a good law.

2702
01:49:37.560 --> 01:49:39.720
It was promoted very heavily by News Corp,

2703
01:49:39.720 --> 01:49:42.093
which is a major media presence there.

2704
01:49:43.800 --> 01:49:46.650
But Facebook's response, Google said, okay, we'll do it.

2705
01:49:46.650 --> 01:49:47.970
And they cut deals with media companies

2706
01:49:47.970 --> 01:49:48.960
to pay them a bunch of money

2707
01:49:48.960 --> 01:49:50.820
for the rights to host their content.

2708
01:49:50.820 --> 01:49:53.400
Facebook said, okay, we won't host any news.

2709
01:49:53.400 --> 01:49:54.870
And they just flipped the switch,

2710
01:49:54.870 --> 01:49:59.580
which blew my mind for a couple of reasons.

2711
01:49:59.580 --> 01:50:02.340
And one was that I remember very clearly,

2712
01:50:02.340 --> 01:50:03.440
three years before that,

2713
01:50:03.440 --> 01:50:05.340
in the middle of the genocide in Myanmar,

2714
01:50:05.340 --> 01:50:07.830
when the United Nations is screaming their heads off

2715
01:50:07.830 --> 01:50:10.020
to say Facebook, you were driving actively

2716
01:50:10.020 --> 01:50:11.790
this ongoing genocide,

2717
01:50:11.790 --> 01:50:14.392
and Facebook refused to turn off the platform,

2718
01:50:14.392 --> 01:50:15.390
and were getting asked by journalists,

2719
01:50:15.390 --> 01:50:18.030
just please, would you consider switching it off?

2720
01:50:18.030 --> 01:50:19.800
And they said, no, we won't do it.

2721
01:50:19.800 --> 01:50:23.040
But when someone targeted their ad revenue in Australia,

2722
01:50:23.040 --> 01:50:25.380
all of a sudden it's lights off,

2723
01:50:25.380 --> 01:50:27.000
and it had a real impact on the country

2724
01:50:27.000 --> 01:50:29.760
because these platforms have been so successful

2725
01:50:29.760 --> 01:50:34.110
at dominating how people consume news and get information.

2726
01:50:34.110 --> 01:50:38.430
All of these like domestic abuse groups

2727
01:50:38.430 --> 01:50:40.230
that were based on Facebook,

2728
01:50:40.230 --> 01:50:42.477
like weather groups couldn't get information out.

2729
01:50:42.477 --> 01:50:43.740
And there are other websites

2730
01:50:43.740 --> 01:50:46.560
that people could obviously access to get the news,

2731
01:50:46.560 --> 01:50:48.120
but of course, once news publishers

2732
01:50:48.120 --> 01:50:49.200
were blocked in Australia,

2733
01:50:49.200 --> 01:50:51.510
what filled in the void for people on there

2734
01:50:51.510 --> 01:50:52.890
who wanted to learn what was going on in the world,

2735
01:50:52.890 --> 01:50:54.660
but rumor and misinformation.

2736
01:50:54.660 --> 01:50:55.493
Sure.

2737
01:50:55.493 --> 01:51:00.380
And so it was a, I think a telling blow that there are,

2738
01:51:01.310 --> 01:51:03.030
or a telling incident that there are gonna be

2739
01:51:03.030 --> 01:51:04.200
more fights like this

2740
01:51:04.200 --> 01:51:06.180
between governments and the major companies,

2741
01:51:06.180 --> 01:51:07.740
which we don't experience here as much

2742
01:51:07.740 --> 01:51:09.180
because it's like unthinkable.

2743
01:51:09.180 --> 01:51:11.430
How could we shut down our own, you know,

2744
01:51:11.430 --> 01:51:14.070
three or four largest companies in the country?

2745
01:51:14.070 --> 01:51:16.830
But in other countries, it's much more live question.

2746
01:51:16.830 --> 01:51:20.280
It was interesting how Facebook got its hold

2747
01:51:20.280 --> 01:51:22.470
in some of these developing countries, right,

2748
01:51:22.470 --> 01:51:26.190
where you describe the, you know,

2749
01:51:26.190 --> 01:51:31.190
distribution of low cost mobile devices that were pre,

2750
01:51:32.760 --> 01:51:36.000
you know, pre-programmed with a rudimentary Facebook app

2751
01:51:36.000 --> 01:51:37.560
already built into them,

2752
01:51:37.560 --> 01:51:42.560
and deals that Facebook struck with cell providers

2753
01:51:43.650 --> 01:51:47.670
to basically give them free data plans for a period of time.

2754
01:51:47.670 --> 01:51:51.240
And so that basically, you know, created a situation

2755
01:51:51.240 --> 01:51:54.780
where their only way of communicating with each other,

2756
01:51:54.780 --> 01:51:56.670
and for purposes of how they're sourcing

2757
01:51:56.670 --> 01:51:59.280
their information and news was through Facebook.

2758
01:51:59.280 --> 01:52:01.530
Right, which they presented at the time

2759
01:52:01.530 --> 01:52:04.110
as this great gift to the world,

2760
01:52:04.110 --> 01:52:05.040
that we're going to go out

2761
01:52:05.040 --> 01:52:08.283
and pay for cell phone internet data.

2762
01:52:08.283 --> 01:52:10.680
Just like the drug dealer giving you the freebie.

2763
01:52:10.680 --> 01:52:12.117
Exactly, right.

2764
01:52:12.117 --> 01:52:13.590
And if you look at the way

2765
01:52:13.590 --> 01:52:14.760
that they would talk about it internally,

2766
01:52:14.760 --> 01:52:16.290
it was all about owning the market.

2767
01:52:16.290 --> 01:52:18.650
It's all about, and this is what they did in Myanmar.

2768
01:52:18.650 --> 01:52:20.310
So people have cell phones for the first time,

2769
01:52:20.310 --> 01:52:21.557
people have internet for the first time.

2770
01:52:21.557 --> 01:52:25.380
We are going to make sure that they 100% access the internet

2771
01:52:25.380 --> 01:52:26.213
through Facebook.

2772
01:52:26.213 --> 01:52:27.960
So that when this market matures

2773
01:52:27.960 --> 01:52:29.010
and becomes worth something--

2774
01:52:29.010 --> 01:52:30.737
They're already so inured to it.

2775
01:52:30.737 --> 01:52:33.450
Right, and they're completely addicted, and we control it,

2776
01:52:33.450 --> 01:52:35.910
and what that, one of the many things that means

2777
01:52:35.910 --> 01:52:39.930
is that local publishers, like let's say a newspaper,

2778
01:52:39.930 --> 01:52:43.380
a media company can't possibly compete, because it's free.

2779
01:52:43.380 --> 01:52:44.430
How can you compete with free,

2780
01:52:44.430 --> 01:52:46.590
and how can you compete with also it's on your phone

2781
01:52:46.590 --> 01:52:48.120
and it's so shiny?

2782
01:52:48.120 --> 01:52:52.650
And they, the displacement that happened

2783
01:52:52.650 --> 01:52:56.250
was just devastating to a lot of reporters,

2784
01:52:56.250 --> 01:52:57.180
a lot of media companies,

2785
01:52:57.180 --> 01:52:59.190
a lot of publishers in these countries.

2786
01:52:59.190 --> 01:53:01.230
And if you talk to people in these countries,

2787
01:53:01.230 --> 01:53:03.635
they think that Facebook is the internet,

2788
01:53:03.635 --> 01:53:06.180
that little F button and what it pulls up,

2789
01:53:06.180 --> 01:53:07.590
they think that's what the internet is.

2790
01:53:07.590 --> 01:53:12.563
So there is really not as much of a standard or practice of,

2791
01:53:14.280 --> 01:53:15.330
well, you have your Facebook,

2792
01:53:15.330 --> 01:53:18.030
but you also go load up the Myanmar Times website,

2793
01:53:18.030 --> 01:53:20.490
or you also go load up these other eight ways.

2794
01:53:20.490 --> 01:53:22.680
You load up your Gmail account to go email with people,

2795
01:53:22.680 --> 01:53:25.200
it's all through these one or two companies

2796
01:53:25.200 --> 01:53:28.500
that control all the information and discussion,

2797
01:53:28.500 --> 01:53:30.360
which is kind of nuts.

2798
01:53:30.360 --> 01:53:33.810
It's really about what sacrifices we're willing to make

2799
01:53:33.810 --> 01:53:35.640
for the sake of convenience, right?

2800
01:53:35.640 --> 01:53:37.920
So when those phones come preloaded with Facebook,

2801
01:53:37.920 --> 01:53:39.810
you can probably go out and get other apps.

2802
01:53:39.810 --> 01:53:43.050
You can, you're free to do what you want with that,

2803
01:53:43.050 --> 01:53:44.910
but when it's so convenient,

2804
01:53:44.910 --> 01:53:48.150
that's gonna create habit formation around that.

2805
01:53:48.150 --> 01:53:50.730
So, and this is playing out for all of us,

2806
01:53:50.730 --> 01:53:53.250
like I had an experience just the other day,

2807
01:53:53.250 --> 01:53:55.890
I went for the first time into an Amazon Fresh.

2808
01:53:55.890 --> 01:53:57.750
Have you had this experience?

2809
01:53:57.750 --> 01:53:58.920
I've been into them once, yeah.

2810
01:53:58.920 --> 01:54:01.260
You have, so I mean, for people that don't know,

2811
01:54:01.260 --> 01:54:04.410
like, okay, pull out your Amazon app on your phone

2812
01:54:04.410 --> 01:54:08.160
and hit the in-store locator thing, it pulls up a QR code.

2813
01:54:08.160 --> 01:54:12.311
And you walk into the place and you scan the QR code,

2814
01:54:12.311 --> 01:54:14.550
and then you just go and put whatever you want in the bag,

2815
01:54:14.550 --> 01:54:15.480
and then you leave.

2816
01:54:15.480 --> 01:54:17.400
And there's, and then you look up at the,

2817
01:54:17.400 --> 01:54:19.800
there's a scaffolding just like we have up here

2818
01:54:19.800 --> 01:54:22.800
in the studio, and there's so many cameras

2819
01:54:22.800 --> 01:54:24.180
that are looking at you.

2820
01:54:24.180 --> 01:54:28.200
There is nothing, I mean, if you, you know, pulled a note,

2821
01:54:28.200 --> 01:54:30.360
a hair out of your nose, it would notice it, right?

2822
01:54:30.360 --> 01:54:33.570
Like, and I had this strange sense of like,

2823
01:54:33.570 --> 01:54:37.672
I am being so hyperwatched right now.

2824
01:54:37.672 --> 01:54:39.390
And you consented to it, you chose that.

2825
01:54:39.390 --> 01:54:40.223
I chose it.

2826
01:54:40.223 --> 01:54:41.460
This is the point that I'm trying to make.

2827
01:54:41.460 --> 01:54:43.710
So I went in, I was like, I wanna see what this is like,

2828
01:54:43.710 --> 01:54:44.877
I went in and then I did it.

2829
01:54:44.877 --> 01:54:48.002
And it is weird to like, okay, I can leave now?

2830
01:54:48.002 --> 01:54:49.620
Like, I think there's that Saturday Night Live skit

2831
01:54:49.620 --> 01:54:52.680
where Keenan, what's his name?

2832
01:54:52.680 --> 01:54:57.480
Keenan Thompson, yeah, he's like there and it's like,

2833
01:54:57.480 --> 01:54:59.370
he thinks he's gonna get arrested 'cause he's black.

2834
01:54:59.370 --> 01:55:00.630
He's like, no, you can leave.

2835
01:55:00.630 --> 01:55:04.107
Like, I was like, am I, what is happening, right?

2836
01:55:04.107 --> 01:55:06.240
And I thought that is incredible,

2837
01:55:06.240 --> 01:55:07.830
it's so unbelievably convenient,

2838
01:55:07.830 --> 01:55:09.335
but am I comfortable with that?

2839
01:55:09.335 --> 01:55:10.950
Like, I don't know, and I keep thinking about it,

2840
01:55:10.950 --> 01:55:13.740
like this is something that that is at the,

2841
01:55:13.740 --> 01:55:15.690
that's like right at that friction point

2842
01:55:15.690 --> 01:55:20.070
of like convenience and the sacrifice of privacy.

2843
01:55:20.070 --> 01:55:23.100
Of course, Amazon is collecting the information

2844
01:55:23.100 --> 01:55:24.060
about what I've bought,

2845
01:55:24.060 --> 01:55:26.700
and is then gonna use that data to serve up

2846
01:55:26.700 --> 01:55:27.848
whatever it's gonna serve up to me.

2847
01:55:27.848 --> 01:55:28.681
Sell you more things.

2848
01:55:28.681 --> 01:55:31.803
Yeah, you're all a pawn in this greater thing, right?

2849
01:55:33.120 --> 01:55:34.500
And that is when I have heard,

2850
01:55:34.500 --> 01:55:36.420
I'm generally skeptical of arguments

2851
01:55:36.420 --> 01:55:39.270
that breaking up the companies will improve things,

2852
01:55:39.270 --> 01:55:40.980
because it does nothing to change

2853
01:55:40.980 --> 01:55:43.500
the underlying business model of maximizing engagement

2854
01:55:43.500 --> 01:55:44.333
to sell ads.

2855
01:55:44.333 --> 01:55:47.010
But one of the versions of the argument

2856
01:55:47.010 --> 01:55:49.440
for breaking up the companies that I have heard

2857
01:55:49.440 --> 01:55:52.440
that does make, I think some logical sense

2858
01:55:52.440 --> 01:55:55.140
is that their enormous market power,

2859
01:55:55.140 --> 01:55:57.930
just the billions and billions of dollars they have,

2860
01:55:57.930 --> 01:55:59.190
and the fact that they control

2861
01:55:59.190 --> 01:56:01.140
all of these different aspects

2862
01:56:01.140 --> 01:56:04.170
of how we relate to the outside world

2863
01:56:04.170 --> 01:56:06.090
and engage with the world around us,

2864
01:56:06.090 --> 01:56:07.470
makes it very easy for them

2865
01:56:07.470 --> 01:56:09.090
to introduce something like this, to say,

2866
01:56:09.090 --> 01:56:10.590
well now there's a store everywhere,

2867
01:56:10.590 --> 01:56:13.290
and it's in every community, and there's all these cameras,

2868
01:56:13.290 --> 01:56:15.120
and you normally would never say yes to it,

2869
01:56:15.120 --> 01:56:16.590
but we're gonna make it so easy,

2870
01:56:16.590 --> 01:56:18.450
and maybe we're gonna give you a little bit of a discount

2871
01:56:18.450 --> 01:56:19.350
for going there,

2872
01:56:19.350 --> 01:56:21.180
so eventually you're going to acclimate to it.

2873
01:56:21.180 --> 01:56:22.080
And that is something,

2874
01:56:22.080 --> 01:56:25.260
I mean, it's like Facebook going into Myanmar for free,

2875
01:56:25.260 --> 01:56:27.420
and saying we're gonna lose money for 20 years

2876
01:56:27.420 --> 01:56:29.760
so that we can own it for the next 100.

2877
01:56:29.760 --> 01:56:31.620
It is interesting.

2878
01:56:31.620 --> 01:56:36.620
I mean, it seems like such an easy lift to say

2879
01:56:36.750 --> 01:56:39.630
that WhatsApp and Instagram

2880
01:56:39.630 --> 01:56:42.120
should be divorced from Facebook, right?

2881
01:56:42.120 --> 01:56:45.450
And to me, the fact that the Justice Department, or the FTC,

2882
01:56:45.450 --> 01:56:49.230
or the Antitrust Department or the Justice Department,

2883
01:56:49.230 --> 01:56:52.350
can't make that case or has not yet made that case.

2884
01:56:52.350 --> 01:56:55.680
Like it feels feckless on my part,

2885
01:56:55.680 --> 01:56:57.000
you know, from my perspective

2886
01:56:57.000 --> 01:57:00.270
that they haven't proceeded in the antitrust actions

2887
01:57:00.270 --> 01:57:02.250
that they have gone, that they have pursued

2888
01:57:02.250 --> 01:57:07.250
seem like weak versions of what the real problem is.

2889
01:57:07.560 --> 01:57:08.790
You mean in terms of changing

2890
01:57:08.790 --> 01:57:09.819
the underlying mechanics of this.

2891
01:57:09.819 --> 01:57:11.473
Yeah, right, yeah, yeah.

2892
01:57:11.473 --> 01:57:14.703
There is this sense where it's,

2893
01:57:15.570 --> 01:57:19.020
it's a blunt instrument for what they're trying to do.

2894
01:57:19.020 --> 01:57:22.350
It's a hammer when maybe you need a scalpel.

2895
01:57:22.350 --> 01:57:25.740
Some of the cases, I know I'm not a lawyer.

2896
01:57:25.740 --> 01:57:28.020
It's tough for me to say how compelling they are or not.

2897
01:57:28.020 --> 01:57:29.550
You would know that better than I would.

2898
01:57:29.550 --> 01:57:31.440
Some of them do, at least people tell me

2899
01:57:31.440 --> 01:57:33.180
that they are proceeding

2900
01:57:33.180 --> 01:57:35.130
and that they might be a little bit more promising

2901
01:57:35.130 --> 01:57:35.963
than we think.

2902
01:57:37.500 --> 01:57:39.690
There's some argument that they work as a deterrent,

2903
01:57:39.690 --> 01:57:42.300
that it's just, you better stay on our good side,

2904
01:57:42.300 --> 01:57:45.240
or else we're gonna bring down this enforcement action but--

2905
01:57:45.240 --> 01:57:46.800
Right, but meanwhile.

2906
01:57:46.800 --> 01:57:49.950
I mean, come on, look at how they continue

2907
01:57:49.950 --> 01:57:53.529
with wild abandoned to build and grow and consolidate.

2908
01:57:53.529 --> 01:57:56.490
Right, it did look around like 2017

2909
01:57:56.490 --> 01:57:57.840
like they were really gonna try

2910
01:57:57.840 --> 01:57:59.190
to make peace with Washington.

2911
01:57:59.190 --> 01:58:01.710
And I actually, as I say that, they really did,

2912
01:58:01.710 --> 01:58:04.410
and they were actually enormously successful

2913
01:58:04.410 --> 01:58:08.130
even as Trump and a lot of senior Republicans

2914
01:58:08.130 --> 01:58:09.390
were railing constantly against

2915
01:58:09.390 --> 01:58:11.100
the sort of social media companies

2916
01:58:11.100 --> 01:58:14.670
had cultivating a lot of people in power in Washington.

2917
01:58:14.670 --> 01:58:15.900
But what they were not prepared for

2918
01:58:15.900 --> 01:58:19.560
was the flip to a Democratic administration.

2919
01:58:19.560 --> 01:58:21.540
Although it's tough for me to say,

2920
01:58:21.540 --> 01:58:23.610
there's a lot of talk

2921
01:58:23.610 --> 01:58:25.350
out of this White House and Justice Department,

2922
01:58:25.350 --> 01:58:29.220
that now that we're in power, we're gonna use our 48 years

2923
01:58:29.220 --> 01:58:31.680
to do the thing that they manage,

2924
01:58:31.680 --> 01:58:35.190
these companies managed to buy a reprieve from in 2016

2925
01:58:35.190 --> 01:58:37.290
when they kind of got Trump on their side.

2926
01:58:38.460 --> 01:58:40.050
I don't know if it's actually gonna happen or not,

2927
01:58:40.050 --> 01:58:40.890
I honestly couldn't say.

2928
01:58:40.890 --> 01:58:42.810
It doesn't feel like it.

2929
01:58:42.810 --> 01:58:44.970
If it is gonna happen, it's happening quietly,

2930
01:58:44.970 --> 01:58:46.800
which may mean it's not happening.

2931
01:58:46.800 --> 01:58:47.670
Yeah.

2932
01:58:47.670 --> 01:58:50.673
So as we kind of start to round this out,

2933
01:58:53.220 --> 01:58:58.140
where do you, if things continue on this trajectory,

2934
01:58:58.140 --> 01:59:00.780
the way that they've been playing out,

2935
01:59:00.780 --> 01:59:04.320
like, what is the, you know, where is this headed?

2936
01:59:04.320 --> 01:59:05.790
Like can you paint the picture

2937
01:59:05.790 --> 01:59:08.100
of the near and distant future?

2938
01:59:08.100 --> 01:59:10.290
So I got, it's hard to say

2939
01:59:10.290 --> 01:59:12.540
partly because the platforms change a lot.

2940
01:59:12.540 --> 01:59:16.530
So we don't have a great sense of TikTok's influence

2941
01:59:16.530 --> 01:59:18.297
on politics and society.

2942
01:59:18.297 --> 01:59:19.590
Well, that's a whole other podcast,

2943
01:59:19.590 --> 01:59:22.260
especially with Chinese ownership, et cetera,

2944
01:59:22.260 --> 01:59:24.270
and the black box nature of it all.

2945
01:59:24.270 --> 01:59:26.933
Right, it's so black box, and the Dynat,

2946
01:59:28.650 --> 01:59:31.470
what it surfaces and doesn't is so different

2947
01:59:31.470 --> 01:59:32.340
from other platforms,

2948
01:59:32.340 --> 01:59:33.480
that we don't have a great sense

2949
01:59:33.480 --> 01:59:35.580
for what the implications of that are

2950
01:59:35.580 --> 01:59:37.740
for users individually or collectively.

2951
01:59:37.740 --> 01:59:39.240
And there could always be another platform

2952
01:59:39.240 --> 01:59:40.800
that has some entirely different effect.

2953
01:59:40.800 --> 01:59:44.730
But I will say that I spent a lot of time for this book

2954
01:59:44.730 --> 01:59:46.110
in Brazil,

2955
01:59:46.110 --> 01:59:49.800
which is a country that is very similar to the United States

2956
01:59:49.800 --> 01:59:52.800
in so many ways in terms of it's politics,

2957
01:59:52.800 --> 01:59:54.840
it's social divisions, it's racial divisions,

2958
01:59:54.840 --> 01:59:56.490
it's economic makeup,

2959
01:59:56.490 --> 02:00:00.000
and felt to me like it is a few years ahead

2960
02:00:00.000 --> 02:00:02.760
of the United States in terms of social media's influence,

2961
02:00:02.760 --> 02:00:05.373
which is just as big there as it is here.

2962
02:00:06.222 --> 02:00:10.050
And I profiled several people

2963
02:00:10.050 --> 02:00:13.770
who were YouTube influencers basically,

2964
02:00:13.770 --> 02:00:16.980
who were now in high positions of government.

2965
02:00:16.980 --> 02:00:20.430
Bolsonaro, the president, he kind of got a start

2966
02:00:20.430 --> 02:00:21.720
as like a YouTube guy.

2967
02:00:21.720 --> 02:00:23.310
He was just like this fringe person

2968
02:00:23.310 --> 02:00:25.890
with very little constituency in the country,

2969
02:00:25.890 --> 02:00:28.050
but a huge influence on YouTube.

2970
02:00:28.050 --> 02:00:32.280
And I think the hints of the future that I got from Brazil

2971
02:00:32.280 --> 02:00:35.970
are that I think the relationship

2972
02:00:35.970 --> 02:00:39.960
between politics and social media is going to flip.

2973
02:00:39.960 --> 02:00:43.890
And I think that we are going to see less of social media

2974
02:00:43.890 --> 02:00:46.530
as a place where some political influence plays out,

2975
02:00:46.530 --> 02:00:48.270
and then feeds into politics,

2976
02:00:48.270 --> 02:00:50.637
some kind of the bottom up in the way that we saw in 2016.

2977
02:00:50.637 --> 02:00:54.720
And I think that we are going to see more politics

2978
02:00:54.720 --> 02:00:58.110
driven actively by and mirroring social media,

2979
02:00:58.110 --> 02:01:00.030
in the way that in Brazil,

2980
02:01:00.030 --> 02:01:02.940
you don't have lawmakers who are trying to appease Facebook.

2981
02:01:02.940 --> 02:01:05.850
You have people from Facebook who are now in government,

2982
02:01:05.850 --> 02:01:07.800
and the constituencies that they're serving

2983
02:01:07.800 --> 02:01:09.180
are the social media algorithms,

2984
02:01:09.180 --> 02:01:11.790
because that's what put them in power in the first place.

2985
02:01:11.790 --> 02:01:13.440
That's interesting.

2986
02:01:13.440 --> 02:01:16.110
It's hard to imagine that here though right now.

2987
02:01:16.110 --> 02:01:17.179
You think so?

2988
02:01:17.179 --> 02:01:18.012
I don't know.

2989
02:01:18.012 --> 02:01:19.566
I don't think we're that far from it.

2990
02:01:19.566 --> 02:01:22.290
I don't think that we're far from,

2991
02:01:22.290 --> 02:01:25.290
I mean, in some ways with the like stop the steel candidates

2992
02:01:25.290 --> 02:01:27.870
and the Republican party who are rising up,

2993
02:01:27.870 --> 02:01:29.250
and some of the QAnon people,

2994
02:01:29.250 --> 02:01:32.070
like these are already people who came from social media

2995
02:01:32.070 --> 02:01:33.182
into politics.

2996
02:01:33.182 --> 02:01:34.015
Right, right, right.

2997
02:01:34.015 --> 02:01:35.370
And I think that that is something that is,

2998
02:01:35.370 --> 02:01:38.160
we're gonna see more capture that in the Republican party.

2999
02:01:38.160 --> 02:01:39.840
I don't know exactly what it would look like

3000
02:01:39.840 --> 02:01:41.790
in the Democratic party, I just can't say,

3001
02:01:41.790 --> 02:01:43.830
but it would not shock me if we saw a version of that

3002
02:01:43.830 --> 02:01:44.663
soon enough.

3003
02:01:45.630 --> 02:01:48.930
And what is that forebode from your perspective?

3004
02:01:48.930 --> 02:01:52.920
I mean when you think about the incentives

3005
02:01:52.920 --> 02:01:55.117
of political leaders, what are they trying to do?

3006
02:01:55.117 --> 02:01:56.250
They're trying to get reelected, right?

3007
02:01:56.250 --> 02:01:59.280
Or they're trying to go from the minority party

3008
02:01:59.280 --> 02:02:01.320
to the majority party.

3009
02:02:01.320 --> 02:02:05.250
And those incentives have been changing in American politics

3010
02:02:05.250 --> 02:02:08.400
for a long time because of the collapse and weakening

3011
02:02:08.400 --> 02:02:10.830
of the party system,

3012
02:02:10.830 --> 02:02:12.510
because of the way that politics

3013
02:02:12.510 --> 02:02:13.860
are increasingly nationalized,

3014
02:02:13.860 --> 02:02:15.270
where you look at your lawmaker

3015
02:02:15.270 --> 02:02:17.610
as a representative of the president's party now,

3016
02:02:17.610 --> 02:02:18.943
instead of as someone who you might have

3017
02:02:18.943 --> 02:02:21.093
a more personal local relationship with.

3018
02:02:22.050 --> 02:02:26.280
But I think we could be facing a much larger flip

3019
02:02:26.280 --> 02:02:30.720
where it's not just that politics are nationalized.

3020
02:02:30.720 --> 02:02:32.430
If you're running for a house seat in Congress,

3021
02:02:32.430 --> 02:02:34.290
you're thinking of, how do I fit into

3022
02:02:34.290 --> 02:02:35.790
the national political conversation,

3023
02:02:35.790 --> 02:02:36.623
but you're thinking,

3024
02:02:36.623 --> 02:02:39.267
how do I fit into this social media conversation?

3025
02:02:39.267 --> 02:02:43.590
And that means how do I get the right number of people

3026
02:02:43.590 --> 02:02:46.290
on Facebook or Twitter or YouTube

3027
02:02:46.290 --> 02:02:48.600
who are gonna be into what I'm doing?

3028
02:02:48.600 --> 02:02:50.880
And that's just, it's a different market.

3029
02:02:50.880 --> 02:02:52.530
It's a different constituency,

3030
02:02:52.530 --> 02:02:55.170
and it's a different set of interests and incentives

3031
02:02:55.170 --> 02:02:57.030
than you have in traditional retail politics.

3032
02:02:57.030 --> 02:02:57.863
Sure.

3033
02:02:57.863 --> 02:03:01.800
You have to understand the vicissitudes of the algorithm

3034
02:03:01.800 --> 02:03:06.800
and play that game, to literally game it in your favor,

3035
02:03:07.020 --> 02:03:10.170
which means it's gonna push both sides towards

3036
02:03:10.170 --> 02:03:12.150
the more radical fringes

3037
02:03:12.150 --> 02:03:16.110
of the respective parties and view,

3038
02:03:16.110 --> 02:03:18.600
and that's gonna then push the constituents

3039
02:03:18.600 --> 02:03:20.280
in that direction as well,

3040
02:03:20.280 --> 02:03:21.780
until we're so far apart

3041
02:03:21.780 --> 02:03:25.590
that it doesn't feel like the nation can cohere.

3042
02:03:25.590 --> 02:03:29.040
I mean, this is the dystopian, you know,,

3043
02:03:29.040 --> 02:03:30.930
end point of this whole thing.

3044
02:03:30.930 --> 02:03:34.290
And it's like insane to even contemplate

3045
02:03:34.290 --> 02:03:36.510
that that could be a reality,

3046
02:03:36.510 --> 02:03:40.200
but it does feel more and more prescient.

3047
02:03:40.200 --> 02:03:42.420
Yeah, I mean, I think it's,

3048
02:03:42.420 --> 02:03:44.490
if that is going to be slowed down,

3049
02:03:44.490 --> 02:03:46.710
that's a change that I think can't just come from

3050
02:03:46.710 --> 02:03:48.720
whatever we do with these technology companies.

3051
02:03:48.720 --> 02:03:51.420
That is something where it's also a symptom of,

3052
02:03:51.420 --> 02:03:52.680
like you were talking about,

3053
02:03:52.680 --> 02:03:54.120
one of the symptoms of extremism

3054
02:03:54.120 --> 02:03:56.520
is people feeling left behind in society.

3055
02:03:56.520 --> 02:03:57.660
This is also a symptom

3056
02:03:57.660 --> 02:04:01.380
of our political systems' gatekeepers really weakening.

3057
02:04:01.380 --> 02:04:04.140
And speaking of whole other podcast,

3058
02:04:04.140 --> 02:04:05.550
like strengthening the party system

3059
02:04:05.550 --> 02:04:07.020
and strengthening political gatekeepers

3060
02:04:07.020 --> 02:04:08.430
is definitely a whole other podcast,

3061
02:04:08.430 --> 02:04:11.550
but that feels to me like the only way

3062
02:04:11.550 --> 02:04:14.850
to kind of slow or even start to reverse this trend.

3063
02:04:14.850 --> 02:04:18.870
Yeah, do you feel like the gatekeepers on Capitol Hill

3064
02:04:18.870 --> 02:04:21.630
are becoming more educated and savvy

3065
02:04:21.630 --> 02:04:26.630
about the perniciousness and ills of social media?

3066
02:04:26.670 --> 02:04:29.070
I mean, we've had, famously there was these hearings,

3067
02:04:29.070 --> 02:04:31.680
but you know, sort of aside from that,

3068
02:04:31.680 --> 02:04:32.513
there was this sense,

3069
02:04:32.513 --> 02:04:35.250
like these guys don't actually really know what's going on.

3070
02:04:35.250 --> 02:04:38.880
So I remember the hearings were some of the questions

3071
02:04:38.880 --> 02:04:40.830
were like, what's a Facebook page?

3072
02:04:40.830 --> 02:04:41.663
How do I post?

3073
02:04:41.663 --> 02:04:43.410
Sir, we sell ads.

3074
02:04:43.410 --> 02:04:44.820
There's that whole thing.

3075
02:04:44.820 --> 02:04:46.530
There has actually been,

3076
02:04:46.530 --> 02:04:49.200
especially in the last like two years,

3077
02:04:49.200 --> 02:04:51.270
and especially after January 6th,

3078
02:04:51.270 --> 02:04:54.390
I think a, a really impressive education on--

3079
02:04:54.390 --> 02:04:55.500
That's encouraging.

3080
02:04:55.500 --> 02:04:56.460
It really is.

3081
02:04:56.460 --> 02:04:59.760
And even the member of the house

3082
02:04:59.760 --> 02:05:01.530
who represents Silicon Valley,

3083
02:05:01.530 --> 02:05:03.990
which I was kind of blown away by, have really come around

3084
02:05:03.990 --> 02:05:06.750
to a much more sophisticated understanding

3085
02:05:06.750 --> 02:05:10.230
of what the roots of the problems are,

3086
02:05:10.230 --> 02:05:14.190
and what pressure points on the company to point to.

3087
02:05:14.190 --> 02:05:18.780
I did a story about YouTube's algorithm,

3088
02:05:18.780 --> 02:05:22.020
plucking out home movies of little kids

3089
02:05:22.020 --> 02:05:23.880
in various states of undressed

3090
02:05:23.880 --> 02:05:25.230
that looked innocent on their own,

3091
02:05:25.230 --> 02:05:26.820
but then stringing them together in a way

3092
02:05:26.820 --> 02:05:29.040
that was meant to sexualize them,

3093
02:05:29.040 --> 02:05:32.100
and to present them as soft core pornography basically.

3094
02:05:32.100 --> 02:05:36.390
And after that came out,

3095
02:05:36.390 --> 02:05:38.400
there were letters from a couple of senators

3096
02:05:38.400 --> 02:05:41.070
that I was kind of blown away by how on point they were,

3097
02:05:41.070 --> 02:05:44.140
where they were demanding Google and YouTube

3098
02:05:45.570 --> 02:05:49.320
change where in the product design process,

3099
02:05:49.320 --> 02:05:53.280
safety and social impact people would be involved,

3100
02:05:53.280 --> 02:05:54.240
which is something that you,

3101
02:05:54.240 --> 02:05:55.890
I wouldn't have even known to ask about,

3102
02:05:55.890 --> 02:05:57.330
but I ran that by someone and they were like,

3103
02:05:57.330 --> 02:05:59.430
wow, this is like, they talked to someone

3104
02:05:59.430 --> 02:06:02.220
maybe probably who left YouTube or left Google.

3105
02:06:02.220 --> 02:06:04.080
But I think they're starting to see

3106
02:06:04.080 --> 02:06:06.780
that they need to understand how the companies work

3107
02:06:06.780 --> 02:06:08.400
and how the companies make decisions,

3108
02:06:08.400 --> 02:06:10.800
and then find little pressure points like this

3109
02:06:10.800 --> 02:06:12.470
that they can try to bring attention to,

3110
02:06:12.470 --> 02:06:14.670
to try to steer them in better directions.

3111
02:06:14.670 --> 02:06:17.670
And how receptive are the powers that be

3112
02:06:17.670 --> 02:06:21.840
at the YouTubes, Facebooks, and Googles to zero, right?

3113
02:06:21.840 --> 02:06:22.673
0%.

3114
02:06:22.673 --> 02:06:23.506
Who's the worst?

3115
02:06:25.680 --> 02:06:27.030
It depends on how you measure it,

3116
02:06:27.030 --> 02:06:28.830
but the answer is YouTube.

3117
02:06:28.830 --> 02:06:31.290
Maybe just everybody who I have talked to,

3118
02:06:31.290 --> 02:06:34.530
if I would ask, which is the one that is most pernicious

3119
02:06:34.530 --> 02:06:35.580
in its social impact,

3120
02:06:35.580 --> 02:06:38.910
and which is the company that is most hostile

3121
02:06:38.910 --> 02:06:41.580
towards social pressures and towards political pressures?

3122
02:06:41.580 --> 02:06:44.330
No matter who I asked, their answer was always YouTube.

3123
02:06:45.570 --> 02:06:46.403
Sorry.

3124
02:06:46.403 --> 02:06:50.370
Yeah, that's just, it's unfortunate, it's unfortunate.

3125
02:06:50.370 --> 02:06:51.900
It doesn't mean that, of course that everything,

3126
02:06:51.900 --> 02:06:52.920
I mean, I watch YouTube.

3127
02:06:52.920 --> 02:06:54.150
Doesn't mean everything on it is bad,

3128
02:06:54.150 --> 02:06:57.120
and it doesn't mean everybody who works there is bad.

3129
02:06:57.120 --> 02:07:01.620
But just the way that the company happens to be structured,

3130
02:07:01.620 --> 02:07:04.800
the way that its systems happen to work,

3131
02:07:04.800 --> 02:07:05.850
their destructiveness,

3132
02:07:05.850 --> 02:07:08.310
maybe because their destructiveness is less apparent

3133
02:07:08.310 --> 02:07:10.020
and has gotten less attention than Facebook.

3134
02:07:10.020 --> 02:07:11.370
That's the argument some people make

3135
02:07:11.370 --> 02:07:13.505
is that they've been allowed to skate a little bit

3136
02:07:13.505 --> 02:07:14.865
and the way that other platforms haven't.

3137
02:07:14.865 --> 02:07:17.790
They're segregated out,

3138
02:07:17.790 --> 02:07:20.070
and they're this kind of like money maker

3139
02:07:20.070 --> 02:07:21.360
for Meta and for Google,

3140
02:07:21.360 --> 02:07:24.090
whereas Facebook is, you know, the main market thing.

3141
02:07:24.090 --> 02:07:27.060
It's like the ATM for Google, right, in many ways.

3142
02:07:27.060 --> 02:07:27.893
Yeah.

3143
02:07:27.893 --> 02:07:30.360
What's interesting is as somebody who's on YouTube

3144
02:07:30.360 --> 02:07:32.700
and you know, we're filming this

3145
02:07:32.700 --> 02:07:34.650
for YouTube right now, right?

3146
02:07:34.650 --> 02:07:37.590
It's a constant conversation that we have here

3147
02:07:37.590 --> 02:07:40.920
about how we position our content on YouTube.

3148
02:07:40.920 --> 02:07:43.470
And of course, I know that if we come up

3149
02:07:43.470 --> 02:07:45.510
with a crazy clickbait title

3150
02:07:45.510 --> 02:07:47.640
and some wacky, you know, thumbnail,

3151
02:07:47.640 --> 02:07:50.970
we'll get more views and I just refuse to do it.

3152
02:07:50.970 --> 02:07:53.310
And yet, and so we suffer the consequences.

3153
02:07:53.310 --> 02:07:56.223
Like we don't get, you know, the views that I think

3154
02:07:56.223 --> 02:07:59.730
that our content deserves by and large,

3155
02:07:59.730 --> 02:08:03.060
because we're opting out of playing that game.

3156
02:08:03.060 --> 02:08:05.820
But I need to sleep at night too, right?

3157
02:08:05.820 --> 02:08:07.830
And so, well, maybe we can,

3158
02:08:07.830 --> 02:08:09.090
so it's a constant thing.

3159
02:08:09.090 --> 02:08:10.410
Like, can we say that?

3160
02:08:10.410 --> 02:08:13.124
Like is that okay with you or is that, you know?

3161
02:08:13.124 --> 02:08:15.330
And it's like, it's not even a conversation I wanna have,

3162
02:08:15.330 --> 02:08:17.250
like I just wanna put, you know,

3163
02:08:17.250 --> 02:08:20.400
episode number, blah, blah, blah, Max Fisher.

3164
02:08:20.400 --> 02:08:24.000
And what's tough is that you can wrestle with

3165
02:08:24.000 --> 02:08:26.430
and torture yourself and make the right choice,

3166
02:08:26.430 --> 02:08:28.140
and someone else is gonna make the wrong one,

3167
02:08:28.140 --> 02:08:31.170
because anybody can put anything on YouTube.

3168
02:08:31.170 --> 02:08:34.890
Someone will go the 10 extra steps be more extreme,

3169
02:08:34.890 --> 02:08:36.300
be more provocative,

3170
02:08:36.300 --> 02:08:39.060
and the algorithm will find them, even if it's, you know,

3171
02:08:39.060 --> 02:08:40.473
someone who is using a $7,

3172
02:08:41.733 --> 02:08:44.610
$7, $70 camera and microphone.

3173
02:08:44.610 --> 02:08:48.191
Yeah, but I still, call me naive, Max--

3174
02:08:48.191 --> 02:08:49.120
Naive.
But I still believe that-

3175
02:08:49.120 --> 02:08:50.550
Yeah, you're calling me naive

3176
02:08:50.550 --> 02:08:52.300
before I've even shared my thought,

3177
02:08:54.000 --> 02:08:57.360
that over the, if you play the long game over the long haul,

3178
02:08:57.360 --> 02:09:01.590
like ultimately, you know, water rises to its own level.

3179
02:09:01.590 --> 02:09:02.460
How do you mean?

3180
02:09:02.460 --> 02:09:05.190
Meaning that if I, like, from my perspective,

3181
02:09:05.190 --> 02:09:09.810
if I keep just trying to put out the best conversations

3182
02:09:09.810 --> 02:09:13.350
that I can, in the nuanced form that they,

3183
02:09:13.350 --> 02:09:14.880
you know, often are,

3184
02:09:14.880 --> 02:09:17.730
that ultimately it will find its audience.

3185
02:09:17.730 --> 02:09:19.860
Sure, yeah that's true, yeah.

3186
02:09:19.860 --> 02:09:22.593
But it won't happen quickly.

3187
02:09:23.561 --> 02:09:24.480
It definitely won't happen as quickly.

3188
02:09:24.480 --> 02:09:26.490
There's no short cutting to that,

3189
02:09:26.490 --> 02:09:28.860
and there's a trust and a faith that comes with that

3190
02:09:28.860 --> 02:09:31.470
that exists outside of any algorithm.

3191
02:09:31.470 --> 02:09:32.820
Right, and I mean,

3192
02:09:32.820 --> 02:09:35.147
you are still finding an audience on YouTube,

3193
02:09:35.147 --> 02:09:37.200
that's partly to the algorithm.

3194
02:09:37.200 --> 02:09:38.667
I mean, it's still helping you.

3195
02:09:38.667 --> 02:09:40.988
And it occasionally, you know, like we'll hit a,

3196
02:09:40.988 --> 02:09:42.720
we've, you know, we've had a couple ones

3197
02:09:42.720 --> 02:09:45.780
that have hit the algorithm lottery and go crazy,

3198
02:09:45.780 --> 02:09:48.300
and then of course you try to reverse engineer it.

3199
02:09:48.300 --> 02:09:49.500
You're like, I don't know why that happened.

3200
02:09:49.500 --> 02:09:50.333
Right, yeah.

3201
02:09:50.333 --> 02:09:53.227
I talked to some political activists who used YouTube,

3202
02:09:53.227 --> 02:09:55.710
and they were, in their view, trying to use it for good,

3203
02:09:55.710 --> 02:09:56.543
but they wanted to say,

3204
02:09:56.543 --> 02:09:57.900
well, we need to reach a lot of people

3205
02:09:57.900 --> 02:09:59.580
because we are trying to, this is in Brazil,

3206
02:09:59.580 --> 02:10:02.280
we're trying to change politics in a healthier direction.

3207
02:10:02.280 --> 02:10:04.410
And one of them was telling me that after like six months,

3208
02:10:04.410 --> 02:10:05.430
they looked up and realized

3209
02:10:05.430 --> 02:10:06.780
that they had endorsed all these views

3210
02:10:06.780 --> 02:10:08.460
that they had hated beforehand,

3211
02:10:08.460 --> 02:10:11.970
because it was just so easy to follow those incentives.

3212
02:10:11.970 --> 02:10:14.730
But you can, I mean, the thing is,

3213
02:10:14.730 --> 02:10:16.470
none of these platforms are all bad,

3214
02:10:16.470 --> 02:10:19.230
and promotion is not inherently bad

3215
02:10:19.230 --> 02:10:22.230
so you can still have a really good podcast that reaches,

3216
02:10:22.230 --> 02:10:24.900
you know, a really great audience on YouTube.

3217
02:10:24.900 --> 02:10:27.960
And does it, in terms of the promotion on YouTube,

3218
02:10:27.960 --> 02:10:30.060
basically for free, which is pretty cool.

3219
02:10:30.060 --> 02:10:31.230
Yeah, 100%.

3220
02:10:31.230 --> 02:10:32.520
I mean, I think that it means

3221
02:10:32.520 --> 02:10:36.240
that you have to shoulder responsibility for how you use it,

3222
02:10:36.240 --> 02:10:38.700
and understanding that you are,

3223
02:10:38.700 --> 02:10:40.350
that the deck is stacked against you,

3224
02:10:40.350 --> 02:10:42.510
because it's so powerful and addictive.

3225
02:10:42.510 --> 02:10:46.890
But to the extent that you can manage to restrict yourself

3226
02:10:46.890 --> 02:10:50.670
or to, you know, come up with rules that work for you,

3227
02:10:50.670 --> 02:10:52.110
it can be a positive.

3228
02:10:52.110 --> 02:10:54.330
My whole career is based on the internet.

3229
02:10:54.330 --> 02:10:56.520
It's on YouTube, and Instagram, and all these places.

3230
02:10:56.520 --> 02:10:59.490
And as a result of Twitter and Instagram,

3231
02:10:59.490 --> 02:11:00.780
I've been able to connect with

3232
02:11:00.780 --> 02:11:03.420
an unbelievable number of extraordinary people

3233
02:11:03.420 --> 02:11:04.620
that have enriched my life.

3234
02:11:04.620 --> 02:11:08.820
So it isn't a black or white thing, but I think it is,

3235
02:11:08.820 --> 02:11:12.360
you know, it is just more important than ever

3236
02:11:12.360 --> 02:11:14.007
for people to realize how powerful it is.

3237
02:11:14.007 --> 02:11:17.310
And as your book so beautifully illustrates,

3238
02:11:17.310 --> 02:11:19.740
it is changing our behavior, whether we like it or not,

3239
02:11:19.740 --> 02:11:21.750
or how resistant we are to that idea,

3240
02:11:21.750 --> 02:11:26.250
because it's an assault on our agency, and sentience,

3241
02:11:26.250 --> 02:11:28.830
and all of that, this is happening.

3242
02:11:28.830 --> 02:11:32.640
And so please, you know, reevaluate your relationship

3243
02:11:32.640 --> 02:11:33.510
with these platforms.

3244
02:11:33.510 --> 02:11:35.430
And maybe you can kind of end this.

3245
02:11:35.430 --> 02:11:37.350
You already alluded to how you've made

3246
02:11:37.350 --> 02:11:40.620
some behavioral modifications, but leave us with, you know,

3247
02:11:40.620 --> 02:11:43.890
some poignant thoughts on just how powerful it is,

3248
02:11:43.890 --> 02:11:47.760
and maybe some other ways in which we can modulate

3249
02:11:47.760 --> 02:11:50.880
or think about our information diet,

3250
02:11:50.880 --> 02:11:55.650
and the extent to which these platforms are useful or not.

3251
02:11:55.650 --> 02:11:58.890
I think that you hit the nail on the head.

3252
02:11:58.890 --> 02:12:02.100
I think that's the dilemma that you described

3253
02:12:02.100 --> 02:12:03.453
is the dilemma, a microcosm.

3254
02:12:03.453 --> 02:12:06.330
It's like food addiction, you still have to eat, right?

3255
02:12:06.330 --> 02:12:09.150
So nobody's gonna be, we're not asking you to be a Luddite,

3256
02:12:09.150 --> 02:12:10.320
I'm not gonna be a Luddite.

3257
02:12:10.320 --> 02:12:13.620
No, I don't, you know, I sometimes will hear people say,

3258
02:12:13.620 --> 02:12:14.640
throw away your smartphone.

3259
02:12:14.640 --> 02:12:16.650
You can't throw away your smartphone.

3260
02:12:16.650 --> 02:12:19.290
You can be like Johann and go to Fire Island for a summer,

3261
02:12:19.290 --> 02:12:21.690
but you know, ultimately he's back on the phone.

3262
02:12:24.870 --> 02:12:28.020
We live in a world that is dominated by these platforms,

3263
02:12:28.020 --> 02:12:29.310
and maybe one day that will change.

3264
02:12:29.310 --> 02:12:31.080
But as an individual living in that world,

3265
02:12:31.080 --> 02:12:33.450
we have to make peace with these systems

3266
02:12:33.450 --> 02:12:35.370
that we are gonna have to use

3267
02:12:35.370 --> 02:12:39.780
to understand the world around us, to relate to people.

3268
02:12:39.780 --> 02:12:42.990
And we're all facing a microcosm of the same dilemma

3269
02:12:42.990 --> 02:12:46.200
that you described, which is how to be moral on them,

3270
02:12:46.200 --> 02:12:50.010
knowing that the platforms make it more appealing,

3271
02:12:50.010 --> 02:12:53.610
and enticing, and rewarding than it's ever been to do things

3272
02:12:53.610 --> 02:12:55.440
that we would otherwise consider immoral.

3273
02:12:55.440 --> 02:12:57.600
And they make it harder and harder

3274
02:12:57.600 --> 02:13:01.560
and more of a disincentive to try to be responsible

3275
02:13:01.560 --> 02:13:04.800
with even just individual words that we might use

3276
02:13:04.800 --> 02:13:06.270
on dashing off a tweet,

3277
02:13:06.270 --> 02:13:09.210
because each of these things that might feel like

3278
02:13:09.210 --> 02:13:11.700
you're just at a stop light and you have a few seconds,

3279
02:13:11.700 --> 02:13:13.020
and you're just gonna dash off a comment,

3280
02:13:13.020 --> 02:13:16.470
but they all play into this broader system.

3281
02:13:16.470 --> 02:13:18.060
They're all training the algorithm,

3282
02:13:18.060 --> 02:13:20.700
they're all training yourself, they're training your peers,

3283
02:13:20.700 --> 02:13:21.930
other users.

3284
02:13:21.930 --> 02:13:25.620
And the, maybe the best thing that you can do,

3285
02:13:25.620 --> 02:13:28.620
I mean, you should try to modulate your usage,

3286
02:13:28.620 --> 02:13:30.600
you should try to be really thoughtful about

3287
02:13:30.600 --> 02:13:32.070
how often do I really have to be on it?

3288
02:13:32.070 --> 02:13:35.850
I post like 5% as much as I used to.

3289
02:13:35.850 --> 02:13:37.800
But probably the best thing you can do

3290
02:13:37.800 --> 02:13:39.480
is just to be really aware about

3291
02:13:39.480 --> 02:13:42.240
when you go to tweet something, or post something,

3292
02:13:42.240 --> 02:13:45.270
or to record something on YouTube, being thoughtful about,

3293
02:13:45.270 --> 02:13:47.640
is this because I think this is a good idea

3294
02:13:47.640 --> 02:13:48.473
and what I wanna do,

3295
02:13:48.473 --> 02:13:51.810
or is it because this giant corporation

3296
02:13:51.810 --> 02:13:53.400
has nudged me towards doing that

3297
02:13:53.400 --> 02:13:56.220
because they wanna make a little bit more money off of me

3298
02:13:56.220 --> 02:13:57.960
and try to addict other users

3299
02:13:57.960 --> 02:13:59.190
to make more money off of them?

3300
02:13:59.190 --> 02:14:03.540
And what do I want my input into this giant system

3301
02:14:03.540 --> 02:14:06.270
that is shaping everything in the entire world to be?

3302
02:14:06.270 --> 02:14:09.420
And conversely, applying that same rubric

3303
02:14:09.420 --> 02:14:10.830
to your consumption,

3304
02:14:10.830 --> 02:14:13.380
like do I really need to watch this video,

3305
02:14:13.380 --> 02:14:15.360
or click on this link or whatever?

3306
02:14:15.360 --> 02:14:18.690
Like I think just being much more mindful

3307
02:14:18.690 --> 02:14:21.960
and responsive versus reactive.

3308
02:14:21.960 --> 02:14:23.130
And there are little tricks you can do.

3309
02:14:23.130 --> 02:14:25.950
You can set up times where your phone

3310
02:14:25.950 --> 02:14:27.180
will shut off certain apps.

3311
02:14:27.180 --> 02:14:29.100
You can turn your phone onto grayscale,

3312
02:14:29.100 --> 02:14:30.990
which is actually incredibly helpful

3313
02:14:30.990 --> 02:14:32.280
in making it less addictive.

3314
02:14:32.280 --> 02:14:34.180
It doesn't take away too much from it.

3315
02:14:36.300 --> 02:14:38.310
But ultimately I think it's just about knowing,

3316
02:14:38.310 --> 02:14:41.400
and it's just about trying to find that will

3317
02:14:41.400 --> 02:14:43.140
to decide what the right thing is gonna be,

3318
02:14:43.140 --> 02:14:43.973
and then to do it,

3319
02:14:43.973 --> 02:14:45.750
even if it might feel really good

3320
02:14:45.750 --> 02:14:47.040
to look at some more tweets.

3321
02:14:47.040 --> 02:14:48.000
It's tough, man.

3322
02:14:48.000 --> 02:14:51.303
My eldest stepson, he is 27 now.

3323
02:14:52.544 --> 02:14:55.800
Him and his brother are among the most analog people

3324
02:14:55.800 --> 02:14:59.370
that I know, which speaks to, you know, that generation,

3325
02:14:59.370 --> 02:15:00.780
has a different, it's interesting.

3326
02:15:00.780 --> 02:15:03.990
Like some young people are more analog

3327
02:15:03.990 --> 02:15:07.680
than a lot of people that are my peers, right?

3328
02:15:07.680 --> 02:15:09.330
So he went and got the Light Phone,

3329
02:15:09.330 --> 02:15:10.200
you know, the Light Phone?

3330
02:15:10.200 --> 02:15:11.033
No.

3331
02:15:11.033 --> 02:15:14.640
So it's a cell phone that comes

3332
02:15:14.640 --> 02:15:17.400
with just a rudimentary stack of apps.

3333
02:15:17.400 --> 02:15:19.597
Like you can listen to podcasts on it.

3334
02:15:19.597 --> 02:15:20.430
Oh, that's cool.

3335
02:15:20.430 --> 02:15:24.426
But it's a black and white like LCD screen.

3336
02:15:24.426 --> 02:15:25.640
Oh I think I have seen this.

3337
02:15:25.640 --> 02:15:27.060
And you can do text,

3338
02:15:27.060 --> 02:15:29.527
but they make it all very hard, right?

3339
02:15:29.527 --> 02:15:31.590
Like, and it's a tiny little thing

3340
02:15:31.590 --> 02:15:33.030
and he's like, this is what I'm doing now.

3341
02:15:33.030 --> 02:15:35.070
Like forget the iPhone.

3342
02:15:35.070 --> 02:15:36.420
And he lasted a couple months,

3343
02:15:36.420 --> 02:15:40.500
but it just became impossible for him to be in the world

3344
02:15:40.500 --> 02:15:43.140
and communicate effectively with people like,

3345
02:15:43.140 --> 02:15:45.210
and he ultimately ended up going back

3346
02:15:45.210 --> 02:15:47.175
because this is how powerful these things are.

3347
02:15:47.175 --> 02:15:48.545
We're tethered to these things.

3348
02:15:48.545 --> 02:15:52.263
I bet that you know exactly where your phone is right now.

3349
02:15:53.520 --> 02:15:54.639
Yeah, I think so.

3350
02:15:54.639 --> 02:15:57.360
Yeah, I bet everybody, I know where my phone is.

